{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage","id":1479349,"title":"ST Theories: Why did magic start to fail in DA Mage?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-04-23T10:40:01-07:00","id":1479349,"title":"ST Theories: Why did magic start to fail in DA Mage?","content":"One of the things that I found while browsing the internet was that a large portion of the world Still believes in magic in some form. At least 30% of americans believe in astrology, 41% believe n psychic powers, 42% believe in some form os new-age spiritual energy(not counting astrology or psychic powers), 85% of the human race is religious, Ireland Still doesn't pave over fairy circles because old beliefs are held by the majority even in public office, etc. And that is all just Modern Belief in the IRL version of earth, which is suppose to be orders of magnitude less superstitious and well adjusted than people in the WOD<br /> <br /> Why was it that in the setting of DA Mage, when superstition and spirituality where still at their Peaks, that magic started &quot;dying&quot;. Why did mages start losing their access to easy immortality? Why did the Scourge/Paradox start raising it's ugly head?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-23T11:04:44-07:00","id":1479352,"title":"","content":"A partial answer to this is that reality is <i>influenced by</i> human belief, but it isn't <i>defined by</i> it: there are factors in okay beyond what humans think. There are different views on what those \u201cother factors\u201d are (for example, Choristers point to the One while Etherites point to the inherent properties of the Ether, a notion that's not <i>too</i> far removed from the Technocracy's belief in the existence of objective laws of reality); but every faction that's being honest pretty much has to acknowledge that such factors <i>exist</i>. Even if they're not <i>absolute</i>; even if, with sufficient effort, these Earthly Foundations <i>can</i> be altered, they <i>are</i> still a thing that can't just be handwaved away. <br /> <br /> Magic started to \u201cfail\u201d in the Dark Ages because the Earthly Foundations shifted. The shift did <i>not</i> come from human beliefs; but it still happened. Why? Who knows? That's why I said that this is only a partial answer. If the Choristers are right that the Earthly Foundations represent the will of the One, then they shifted in the Dark Ages because the One willed it. If the Etherites are right about the Earthly Foundations are fundamental properties on the Ether, then the shift in the Dark Ages is evidence that the properties of the Ether can evolve over time. Other explanations of what the Earthly Foundations are will give other reasons why they shifted in the Dark Ages. And this being Mage, there's probably no way to single out one of these explanations as \u201cthe one true answer\u201d.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-23T12:57:18-07:00","id":1479364,"title":"","content":"It's also worth considering that magic didn't actually &quot;fail.&quot;<br /> <br /> One of the changes in human culture, esp. in Europe, at the time was a practical lessening in the power the elite had over the lower classes. Farmers continued to farm largely the same they had been for generations, while kings and aristocrats saw the reach of their power shrink rather drastically. The end of the &quot;dark ages&quot; saw the beginning of what we'd now call the middle class: people without overt political authority, but as a group enough importance economically and socially to force the ruling elite into concessions.<br /> <br /> Most mages at the time built their philosophies around older concepts of hierarchy. Gods, spirits, divine law, and so on, even for the Fellowships like the Spirit Talkers that didn't care about empires, inspire a conceptualization of society that is very structured. And tends to put mages at the top of that structure. Mages that refused to adapt saw their magic get harder. As Data pointed out in a more grand fashion, things shifted. But individual magical practices failing because the mages didn't account for changing conditions doesn't mean magic itself &quot;failed.&quot; That's just the sort of hubris/arrogance mages are somewhat famous for having talking.<br /> <br /> If you look at the transition from the Dark Ages setting to the Sorcerer's Crusade setting... magic in many ways got better. It might not have been as easy for individual mages to master, but magic flourished among even more mages. For the dominant mages in any given area, magic wasn't harder to do, it was only if you tried to go somewhere else that suddenly things got weird for you. <br /> <br /> There's also some intention from mages there. The Order of Reason was trying to create a &quot;pure&quot; magic that was based solely on those Earthly foundations and was experimenting with how to bring that about with all of the older stodgy traditionalists wanting to keep doing what they always did around. The splintering of Reality, and then the spread and consolidation of Consensus, was not just some fluke alternation of the universal rules... it was perhaps the greatest feat of magic ever performed: literally changing how magic itself worked. If you look at the modern Technocracy did magic... ahem... Enlightened Science, actually fail? Or did it simply bend to a centuries long effort to make it the providence of a new group of elites? The Union might not have everything they wanted, but it's hard to say they failed many of their objectives for taking control of things.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-23T14:21:02-07:00","id":1479372,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479364#post1479364\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">snip</div> </div> </div> </div>Wouldn't that essentially mean that modern paradox isn't being caused by consensus, but by a Massive and Ongoing magical ritual built to alter the earthly foundations that concern magic itself? Hopefully they made sure said ritual was excluded from the new rules(imagine the paradox backlash....)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-23T15:29:11-07:00","id":1479375,"title":"","content":"You say that like they're ultimately different things. Modern paradox is caused by consensus, which is the product of the last few centuries of OoR/TU magical influence to alter the way humanity perceives the Earthly Foundations; and thus possibly be shifting those foundations themselves.<br /> <br /> Consider the physics of dropping a steel ball from a great height that impacts the Earth. Did the ball hit the Earth? Did the Earth hit the ball? Which caused the damage both suffered?<br /> <br /> While we tend to see things from a certain perspective (namely because the Earth is so massive and changes so little compared to its totality we view it as a static non-actor), in physics collisions are a mutual affair. Both bodies involved impart energy to each other, in one giant net event of various forces.<br /> <br /> You can't separate things out. The end result of a steel ball impacting the Earth requires the ball, the Earth, and the initial push that causes the impact to happen. Likewise you don't have modern paradox-causing consensus without the magic that went into reshaping humanity's relationship with magic in the first place.<br /> <br /> Though you can't call the Union's efforts &quot;one&quot; ongoing ritual. It's the impact of centuries of magical and mundane influence on the world combined. That's why the Traditions can't just change Consensus with more magic. The OoR might have demonstrated that it can be done, and the Union might have executed a specific outcome, but look at how much work that took to do.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-23T16:10:44-07:00","id":1479379,"title":"","content":"And part of my original point is that not all of the changes that have occurred can be attributed to the Union, or to any other group of mages. That's <i>another</i> part of Mage hubris: the idea that everything revolves around their Magick. <br /> <br /> I mentioned the Etherites and Choristers' views in my first post; let me now point out another \u201cplayer in the game\u201d that might be used to explain the Earthly Foundations: <br /> <br /> The Triat. <br /> <br /> Mage isn't centered around the Wyld, Weaver, and Wyrm the way Werewolf is. But it is entirely possible that the appearance of Paradox and Scourge in the Sorcerers Crusade era <i>could have been</i> because the Weaver decided to step up its \u201ccalcify reality\u201d agenda, and the Wyrm stepped up its \u201ctear down reality\u201d agenda. Under this view, the Earthly Foundations started to shift because the Weaver and the Wyrm took action. <br /> <br /> Which, again, shouldn't be taken as <i>the</i> explanation. I point it out solely to illustrate <i>one possible</i> factor outside of the Consensus and Magickal community that WoD fans would have no problem seeing as responsible for the shift. It <i>could</i> be that much of the Technocracy's wild success can be attributed to them having \u201cthe wind at their backs\u201d, as it were, with the Weaver coincidentally shifting reality in the same direction that <i>they</i> were trying to shift it. <br /> <br /> \u201cThere are more things in Heaven and on Earth, Horatio, than are dreamt of in your philosophy.\u201d \u2014 Hamlet","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-23T19:29:49-07:00","id":1479386,"title":"","content":"Sure, the same way it's entirely possible Lucifer did it or God did it or Ma'at did it.<br /> <br /> But in Mage the most likely and supported reason is the Order of Reason causing big enough changes in the world.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27335-technoma","name":"Technoma","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"103"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-23T20:27:23-07:00","id":1479387,"title":"","content":"Another issue to look at is population. According to one of the Player Guides (I don't have the book at hand) Magic was easier when the Earth had only Five million people ( circa 5000BCE). A few millennia later, when world population was about fifty million, magic got harder. Around 1,000CE, when the Earth had three hundred million people Magick got harder again. <br /> <br /> Paradox and the Scourge show up at a time when the Earth is getting close to half a billion people and Europe is having a population boom. Paradox becomes nearly universal on Earth when the population gets to two billion. <br /> <br /> Population pressure creates a vast sea of conflicting wills stressing reality.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-23T22:41:34-07:00","id":1479399,"title":"","content":"I remember something from Vampire 2nd Edition concerning House Tremere's magic beginning to falter: it was attributed dually to the forces of <i>dominion</i> and <i>reason</i>. The latter can be seen in the Order of Reason's efforts to replace superstition with science and rationality.<br /> <br /> As for the former, in the centuries following Rome's fall Christianity became the dominant religion across Europe at the same time that Islam spread over the Middle East and North Africa. Unlike the pagan religions before them, these two postulated monotheism and were very hostile to witchcraft. The old gods, spirits, and practices that were formerly supported by human belief were now being opposed by them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-23T23:53:44-07:00","id":1479405,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matt the Bruins fan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479399#post1479399\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for the former, in the centuries following Rome's fall Christianity became the dominant religion across Europe at the same time that Islam spread over the Middle East and North Africa. Unlike the pagan religions before them, these two postulated monotheism and were very hostile to witchcraft. The old gods, spirits, and practices that were formerly supported by human belief were now being opposed by them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Wouldn't this mean that many religion based magic organizations like the celestial chorus should have Benefited from the changes? Pagan magic practices would have suffered, but monotheistic magic like faith healing and exorcisms should have Skyrocketed in power if this was a major factor.<br /> <br /> In the OP I mentioned how 85% of the modern world is still religious and considering the rise of places like the bible belt or Multi-million person Mega-churches, the celestial chorus would be rocking massive reality zones that would let them pull any paradox-free miracles they felt like.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-24T01:54:50-07:00","id":1479407,"title":"","content":"A few things to keep in mind:<br /> <br /> 1) Not every mage that used Christian magic was a Messianic Voice that would then go on to join the Celestial Chorus. The Order of Reason drew large numbers of Christian mages into its ranks that weren't purged until the OoR became the Technocracy (and even then the Union still has considerable members of faith).<br /> <br /> 2) Building off the end of that, most people are still members of an organized religion, but that doesn't mean people's relationship to religion is the same as it used to be. People want the spirituality and community of religion, but less and less people are coming to religion as the Ultimate Source of Truth. Just because 80% of people in the US are Christians doesn't mean 80% of people in the US believe that if you pray hard enough to Jesus a preacher can lay their hands on the infirm and heal them with divine spirit. There's also a large diversity of Christians in the US that have various theological differences. That 85% figure is great for explaining why the supernatural continues to exist in the WoD despite the Union's best efforts, but the huge and contradictory set of beliefs under that figure also means there's a lack of coherent alternative. Yes, there a decent number of religion based Reality Zones around, but that doesn't mean they're all that large. And they are most certainly not mutually compatible; a Hindu Reality Zone isn't going to do a Jewish mage much good.<br /> <br /> 3) As I said before, the Sorcerer's Crusade era was not a bad time for organized religion focused mages. But going back to #1, that doesn't mean they sided with the Traditions in the end. Real history saw the shift of people wanting the fickle miracles of the divine to be replaced by the reliable miracles of human understanding.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-24T12:19:39-07:00","id":1479434,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479407#post1479407\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's also a large diversity of Christians in the US that have various theological differences. That 85% figure is great for explaining why the supernatural continues to exist in the WoD despite the Union's best efforts, but the huge and contradictory set of beliefs under that figure also means there's a lack of coherent alternative. Yes, there a decent number of religion based Reality Zones around, but that doesn't mean they're all that large. And they are most certainly not mutually compatible; a Hindu Reality Zone isn't going to do a Jewish mage much good.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not so sure I agree with your second point. I agree that many people of the same faith don't necessarily have the same beliefs, those that do tend to gravitate toward each other into the same communities. Those communities aren't as small as people tend to think either.<br /> <br /> As for modern people Not looking to religion for the Ultimate Truth and not looking to priests for blatant miracles, I can 100% confirm that you are incorrect. I was Raised in one of those communities and I've been brought up by people who looked down on science because they believed their religion was the One Truth. If you look into the mentioned Mega-churches I mentioned, you'll find Hundreds of Thousands of people looking to Priests to perform true Miracles in front of them(and the con artists deliver by taking advantage of the power of belief to make it look like they can do Magic). We had honest to truth thousands of people in one building thinking they were immune to the recent Plague because they thought it was a punishment from heaven. You really underestimate the shear disconnect from reality we have in a lot of modern America alone.<br /> <br /> This is all in a world Without magic. In mage, that place would be a reality zone the size a City where True Magick could be used with impunity, while doing so would only reinforce and Spread the reality zone as more faithful came to see blatant and obvious miracles being performed.<br /> <br /> This is part of the issue I have with mage's consensus. If consensus was the major determining factor is making paradox a thing, then the traditions would have won back reality a long time ago. Something else has to be the major deciding factor or the system falls apart.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-24T13:32:38-07:00","id":1479439,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is all in a world Without magic. In mage, that place would be a reality zone the size a City where True Magick could be used with impunity, while doing so would only reinforce and Spread the reality zone as more faithful came to see blatant and obvious miracles being performed.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think there's a problem with postulating that most people on our world believe in magick - and thus people on WoD should because it's based on our world - and then postulate that WoD it's <i>not our world </i>but a world where magick exists, so obvioulsy people should believe in magick more (Plus, you're assuming our world hasn't blatant miracles and real magick - so insensitive :P)<br /> <br /> If WoD it's based on our world, to the point we have to accept most people believe on one or another form of magic because that's what RL statistics say, then I want to state also that they shouldn't believe &quot;much more&quot; to make sense (otherwise one could argue that they should believe &quot;much less&quot;, instead, to make sense)<br /> <br /> I think the best it's to assume that the state of the WoD that's on the books it's the &quot;base state of reality&quot; - of how it <i>became </i>that way there's countless possibilities that aren't canon. So, If WoD it's based on our own modernity, then people believes just as much as on our world, and not much more nor much less.<br /> <br /> Which means, among other things, that trough christians definitely believe that God and Angels can do, and provide with, teleportation...most believers still will believe - on WoD -that taking a car to go fast somewhere it's more sane than praying to do that. And simply because of that I would say &quot;cars to travel&quot; it's a solid part of the Consensus even if people doesn't beleive on the fundamental scientific principles behind them - where as teleportation prayers are not.<br /> <br /> And that to my central point: &quot;Believing in magic&quot; or &quot;being religious&quot; are tremendously vague terms that explain very little because believing on some effects it's not the same as believing on every effect the same way, and on the same level:<br /> <br /> Like: Why believing that someone can heal with prayer would imply that someone can or will become immortal with them?. Even when God *can* do that, it's also the fate of man to die - and that's *also* something God did, as a punishment to human sin: Resisting it would be Hubris. It tipically implies having pacts with demons, or some sort of divine punishment, and that it's superstition (albeit a common one) and not something the religion actually endorses. One could argue that such beliefs are *against* the idea of any *human* Practice - short of dying and going to Heaven - allowing one to become immortal, adding a layer of passive Will to &quot;the Consensus&quot; to counter such attempts, that only grows with such religious beliefs<br /> <br /> In my oppinion, even if there *wasn't* a Foundation about that, it's debatable if most people are <i>really </i>that supportive of the idea - even among people of cultures that do believe in immortality and immortals. It may very well be that the belief in &quot;death as an inevitability of the human condition&quot; it's superior to the idea of &quot;hey, if you cultivate a lot you could become immortal like the monkey king&quot;.<br /> <br /> And there probably IS a Foundation about that. And a rather strong one since the effect it's often described as having it's own Paradox rules beyond merely being Vulgar.<br /> And I would say that perhaps Earth was <i>always </i>somewhat against Immortality - even if perhaps less so. Those effects were <i>always </i>a bit more complicated than your average Effect, or weren't they?. Check powerful mages from eras previous to that of Tremere: Mages always died, and mages that didn't had a tendecy to become gods or otherwise non-human. DA didn't invented death among mages, nor was the point where mages suddenly stopped being immortal.<br /> <br /> Hermetic vials were becoming more *costly* on a world where Quintessence was becoming rarer than when the Order was founded (which probably *does* have to do with events beyond magely influence, or the Consensus, or even the Foundations), but they were <i>already </i>more costly than your average magick. There was never a point in time where mages didn't tought about immortality after a few centuries of life (the first century or two are easier) as a big deal. At least not one that I had found serious proof of.<br /> <br /> Even more, it could well be that the idea of becoming &quot;immortal like the monkey king&quot;, or Jesus, requires one to stop being a mere human. Maybe that's why so many ancient mages conquered death by becomming gods or otherwhise non human:<br /> A Foundation could be kicking mages from humanity, forcing them to turn to non-human magics or do shenanigans (spend loads of Quintessence, becoming undead, go to the other worlds, etc...), if they wanna become immortal. That, in turn, would reinforce a Consensus about how immortality it's gained and it's consequences - and not just (or even specially) among Sleepers but also within the very Focuses mages use to pursue it.<br /> <br /> And that's just one effect, albeit a very important one. <hr /><br /> Ultimately, the answer as was stated by many, it's that the world changed in many ways and that shifted magick arround.<br /> <br /> And I would argue that's not the first time, even. Tremere just wasn't old enough to remember how magick was on the Roman Empire, much less to know how magick was when &quot;Spheres&quot; were invented for the first time on Egypt (which we don't have a system for, but it's part of the Lore). And imagine if trully legendary times, with legendary characters like the Golden People or the Wyck, existed on the past.<br /> <br /> Reasons are unknown, and could include any or all of the given in this thread. And then some","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-24-2022, 04:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-24T13:53:39-07:00","id":1479441,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479434#post1479434\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Those communities aren't as small as people tend to think either.</div> </div> </div> </div>Do you any particularly case to make in which side of this perception is more subject to perceptional bias? Single data points of large religious communities doesn't demonstrate that most fundamentalist communities are large. That's like saying New York and Los Angeles exist, therefore US cities are underestimated in how large their populations are.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for modern people Not looking to religion for the Ultimate Truth and not looking to priests for blatant miracles, I can 100% confirm that you are incorrect. </div> </div> </div>Please do not turn a conditional statement (&quot;less&quot;) into an absolute one (&quot;not&quot;) to attempt to proclaim me incorrect.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I was Raised in one of those communities and I've been brought up by people who looked down on science because they believed their religion was the One Truth. </div> </div> </div>So, what have you done to separate your biases based on where you grew up to look at the actual data on how the population as a whole is moving regarding their feelings towards religion?<br /> <br /> I live in the kind of place you're talking about. I know what it's like. I've also seen how people that grew up in my town that aren't caught up in the fundamentalist groups also perceive an outsized reach that's born of factors that have nothing to do with faith (like political apathy letting highly motivated fundies dominate local right wing politics).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you look into the mentioned Mega-churches I mentioned, you'll find Hundreds of Thousands of people looking to Priests to perform true Miracles in front of them(and the con artists deliver by taking advantage of the power of belief to make it look like they can do Magic). </div> </div> </div>So, again, lets cut through the bias here.<br /> <br /> ~85% of US citizens belong to an organized religion.<br /> ~1.5% of US citizens attend a megachurch.<br /> <br /> There are over 1,300 megachurches in the US that divvy up that tiny fraction of religious citizens. Only 50 of them claim congregations of 10K or greater (which means ~20% of adherents to such systems are concentrated in just 50 churches, making the vast majority of them much smaller affairs). And many of them inflate the perception of their size by having multiple locations. Having 50K members of your church isn't as impressive when it's spread out over twelve churches across a large geographic area.<br /> <br /> When you drill down into these numbers, megachurches are almost always a minority in a given area. Concentrating bunch of fundies in one building so they have thousands of people together at once, in a town where everyone else is still five times as many people means 80% of the population doesn't think that way. Consensus favors the majority. It is really not hard to show that most megachurches would not result in &quot;city&quot; sized Reality Zones.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is part of the issue I have with mage's consensus. If consensus was the major determining factor is making paradox a thing, then the traditions would have won back reality a long time ago. Something else has to be the major deciding factor or the system falls apart. </div> </div> </div>Um... The Technocracy's efforts to globalize Consensus so no one concentrated community can ever get too powerful?<br /> <br /> Because the Sorcerer's Crusade era was the time where that's how things worked. Consensus was a local affair with major shifts between geography thanks to large Reality Zones. The Union spent centuries using imperialism and colonialism to stamp that out and get one dominant Consensus that wiped out the boundaries that used to exist. Now only pockets of those formerly huge Reality Zones exist.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-24T14:03:54-07:00","id":1479443,"title":"","content":"The upcoming <b>Operative's Dossier</b> has a system in it for Terranorming which could be interpreted as something like this: as written, it requires the use of a source of Primal Energy, such as a Venture, that can be dedicated to the Terranorming. This is as opposed to the \u201cShifting the Zone\u201d rules found in <b>M20</b>. One way to interpret this is that the <b>M20</b> corebook's rules represent shifting the Zone purely by manipulating the Consensus, while the Terranorming rules in the <b>Operative's Dossier</b> deal with altering the underlying Reality Zone. <br /> <br /> In a nutshell, the Terranorming process books down to: \u201cReprime\u201d the Zone by dedicating a suitably large source of Primal Energy to the task; this \u201csoftens\u201d reality (the book's phrasing), which I interpret as making the Zone a bit more subject to change than they otherwise would be. Then you clear the area of disruptive influences, hold it for a length of time based on the size of the Venture that's needed (from one month for a one-dot Venture to half a century for a five-dot Venture) before capping it, \u201csealing\u201d the new reality. (I have submitted errata to the effect that the \u201cShifting the Zone\u201d guidelines in M20 should be referenced here; I'd put them just before the \u201cHolding\u201d step. We'll see if the errata makes it into the final book.)<br /> <br /> This notion of using Primal Ventures as a mechanism for facilitating manipulation of Reality Zones could be expanded on. The Terranorming process is devoted to bringing about change; but I could also see a slight variant of it being used to \u201creinforce\u201d the Zone, \u201chardening\u201d the reality and reducing the influence of the Consensus on it. This would make that Reality Zone more \u201cobjective\u201d*, downplaying the role of human beliefs in determining what's Vulgar and what's Coincidental. <br /> <br /> This notion of \u201csoft reality\u201d vs. \u201chard reality\u201d could also be associated with the rules for Paradox: if a RZ is sufficiently \u201csoft\u201d, everything's Coincidental, and Paradox is not really a thing. As things \u201charden\u201d, more things become Vulgar, and/or the rules for Paradox become harsher: if a RZ gets \u201chard\u201d enough, you might start bringing in some of the more severe Paradox rules from MRev. <br /> <br /> That said, the primary purpose of this notion of the \u201chardness\u201d of a RZ is to address the notion of how well or poorly the nature of the Zone reflects the local Consensus: in a sufficiently \u201csoft\u201d RZ, the nature of the Zone <i>must</i> conform to the Consensus; as it grows harder, discrepancies between the two become more and more possible. <br /> <br /> Historically, the \u201cfailing\u201d of Magic during the Dark Ages involved a gradual \u201chardening\u201d of reality, until you reach the state of things described in Sorcerers Crusade. And that \u201chardening\u201d <i>doesn't</i> have to be entirely, or even mostly, the result of human activity (Sleeper or Awakened). <br /> <br /> <hr /><br /> <span style=\"color:gray\">* I put \u201cobjective\u201d in quotes, because my earlier point was that the nature of the Earthly Foundations isn't known: they <i>could</i> just be impersonal cosmic forces, or they might be the Enlightened Will of Greater Spirit Entities (<b>M20</b> p.487). But <i>whatever</i> their origin, a \u201chardened\u201d Reality Zone would owe more of its rules to them than to the Consensus.</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-24T14:18:41-07:00","id":1479445,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479439#post1479439\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think there's a problem with postulating that most people on our world believe in magick - and thus people on WoD should because it's based on our world - and then postulate that WoD it's <i>not our world </i>but a world where magick exists, so obvioulsy people should believe in magick more (Plus, you're assuming our world hasn't blatant miracles and real magick - so insensitive :P)</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe arguing WOD people should be more superstitious could be bad logic, I disagree, but it ultimately doesn't matter, my point stays the same.<br /> <br /> If the beliefs in WOD were just a copy of IRL, then there are enough people IRL that believe in magic to create Very Large reality zones that wouldlet the tradition get away with vulgar magic without paradox. That was the center of my point. The consensus isn't enough to make the mage world what it is.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479439#post1479439\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Which means, among other things, that trough christians definitely believe that God and Angels can do, and provide with, teleportation...most believers still will believe - on WoD -that taking a car to go fast somewhere it's more sane than praying to do that. And simply because of that I would say &quot;cars to travel&quot; it's a solid part of the Consensus even if people doesn't beleive on the fundamental scientific principles behind them - where as teleportation prayers are not.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't understand your point here? Most people don't believe they're saints that can call on miracles down from on high, true, but that they still believe that certain priests can. I'm not joking. There are massive churches where thousands of people go to witness priests perform miracles they genuinely believe are real.<br /> <br /> Many a celestial chorus member could stand on that platform and go ham because the entire place would be a reality zone made specifically for their paradigm(IF the consensus was what was stopping them, the fact that they don't implies consensus isn't the main factor).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479439#post1479439\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Like: Why believing that someone can heal with prayer would imply that someone can or will become immortal with them?. Even when God *can* do that, it's also the fate of man to die - and that's *also* something God did, as a punishment to human sin: Resisting it would be Hubris. It tipically implies having pacts with demons, or some sort of divine punishment, and that it's superstition (albeit a common one) and not something the religion actually endorses. One could argue that such beliefs are *against* the idea of any *human* Practice - short of dying and going to Heaven - allowing one to become immortal, adding a layer of passive Will to &quot;the Consensus&quot; to counter such attempts, that only grows with such religious beliefs</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm lost as to you point here? On one hand, I actually think I kind of disagree, but I'm not sure I'm understanding you correctly.<br /> <br /> Taking the point to a different context. If you took a cancer patient to a church and a priest says he'll heal the patient using the power of god, would you say most people would believe them?<br /> <br /> Rationally, no they shouldn't. People should know faith healing doesn't trump medical knowledge. Unfortunately reality isn't rational and what often happens is the patient is separated entirely from proper medical care as the person goes almost exclusively to faith healing, to the cheers of the other members in their church. I've been where this kind of thing happens.<br /> <br /> People are 100% willing to believe things that sound impossible or crazy if a person that they view as an authority figure is the one that tells it to them. Doubly so if they have a pre-existing beleif that lines up with what the authority figure says.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479439#post1479439\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">immortality<br /> <br /> Ultimately, the answer as was stated by many, it's that the world changed in many ways and that shifted magick arround.<br /> <br /> And I would argue that's not the first time, even. Tremere just wasn't old enough to remember how magick was on the Roman Empire, much less to know how magick was when &quot;Spheres&quot; were invented for the first time on Egypt (which we don't have a system for, but it's part of the Lore). And imagine if trully legendary times, with legendary characters like the Golden People or the Wyck, existed on the past.<br /> <br /> Reasons are unknown, and could include any or all of the given in this thread. And then some</div> </div> </div> </div>Isn't this exactly what I was saying though? Consensus couldn't be the only factor and I went a step further to say that consensus might not even have been very important in the first place.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479441#post1479441\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, again, lets cut through the bias here.<br /> <br /> ~85% of US citizens belong to an organized religion.<br /> ~1.5% of US citizens attend a megachurch.<br /> <br /> There are over 1,300 megachurches in the US that divvy up that tiny fraction of religious citizens. Only 50 of them claim congregations of 10K or greater (which means ~20% of adherents to such systems are concentrated in just 50 churches, making the vast majority of them much smaller affairs). And many of them inflate the perception of their size by having multiple locations. Having 50K members of your church isn't as impressive when it's spread out over twelve churches across a large geographic area.<br /> <br /> When you drill down into these numbers, megachurches are almost always a minority in a given area. Concentrating bunch of fundies in one building so they have thousands of people together at once, in a town where everyone else is still five times as many people means 80% of the population doesn't think that way. Consensus favors the majority. It is really not hard to show that most megachurches would not result in &quot;city&quot; sized Reality Zones.</div> </div> </div> </div>I should Apologize, I let my personal biases get in the way of the discussion.<br /> <br /> Granted, how large Would a reality zone created by a mega-church be? I'm guessing it would depend as much on whether it was located closer or farther away from the center of a city as it would it's population.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479443#post1479443\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Historically, the \u201cfailing\u201d of Magic during the Dark Ages involved a gradual \u201chardening\u201d of reality, until you reach the state of things described in Sorcerers Crusade. And that \u201chardening\u201d <i>doesn't</i> have to be entirely, or even mostly, the result of human activity (Sleeper or Awakened).<br /> <br /> <hr /><br /> <span style=\"color:gray\">* I put \u201cobjective\u201d in quotes, because my earlier point was that the nature of the Earthly Foundations isn't known: they <i>could</i> just be impersonal cosmic forces, or they might be the Enlightened Will of Greater Spirit Entities (<b>M20</b> p.487). But <i>whatever</i> their origin, a \u201chardened\u201d Reality Zone would owe more of its rules to them than to the Consensus.</span></div> </div> </div> </div>This is the explanation I like the best. Hard and soft reality zones that may or may not line up with consensus does a lot to clear up not just this, but other issues in Mage's setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":6383,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","user":"Prometheas","date":".  More people commenting in the time it took me to type 04-24-2022, 05:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-24T16:31:16-07:00","id":1479450,"title":"","content":"I tend to adopt the same ideas of structural but also 'human/demographic' changes being equally factors, but those are honestly arbitrary boundaries and shaped by my preference to tie everything together even though various ST settings don't *quite* fit perfectly.<br /> <br /> 'Structural' basically means the way the world (or cosmos) is set up. One major 'structural' factor I think is that many systems treat the cosmos (including time) as cyclical. KOTE was the biggest influence here, but I think ti can apply to others (since there were times Gehenna was *also* cyclical). On a cycle you're going to have different changes to the environment and that will include in the ecosystem. Energy may change in many ways but you'll always have a high point, a low point, and periods in between. In an ideal system this might continue indefinitely (or just a very long time given entropy) But many settings (and here I borrow more from DtF, CtD, WtA and not just KOTE) have the world corrupted or fundamentally damaged in some way. It's a world that isn't perfect and it probably doesn't renew itself in the way originally intended (either entropy became a thing, or it accelerated because of the imperfections.)<br /> <br /> Under that assumption, each 'cycle' could mean the energy available to the system decreases (maybe it gets stored someplace it wasn't intended, and it causes further problematic effects. There are lots of ways you could reflect this - the problems with the underworld, creating factors that disrupted the Triat, etc.) and the ecosystem will likewise start to change. Eventually you might get certain magical flora, fauna or resources that get consumed or never replenish or may go extinct because they fail to adapt to changing circumstances (and become bygones). Some areas of the world/cosmos will be less affected by this than others obviously so you can have areas that more magical than others (ie Horizon, or various places on Earth that become nodes, caerns, etc. Another possible side effect of this is that 'pure' quintessence may get bound up in forms that are less easily accessible or renewable (matter, or even just different kinds of energy. Chi, Mana, Gnosis, etc.)<br /> <br /> An imperfect environment might be bad enough by itself, but human nature could likely make it worse (kinda like real life - collectively humans don't do well planning long term, we don't always conserve or adopt sustainable practices when we should, we easily ignore subtle and gradual changes and resist making long term, systemic changes that can require effort. WoD humans are likely to be *worse* rather than better.) Consensus plays a big role here, but human habits and those of our offshoots (magical or otherwise) also play a role. More people, the crystallization of belief in certain ways (that might be toxic to other things - wonder, awe, creativity, etc.) and accelerate a pre-existing downward-spiral Vampires even be a non-trivial 'human' factor - humans played a role in the creation of vampires, and vampires are one of the few supernatural entities (apart from the dead or zombies) who might be said to have been prolfiic to any degree. Nevermind the worm, demons, the Akuma, etc. - Vampires probably can be both the most mundane of supernatural threats and the serious in terms of binding up dwindling reserves of supernatural power in less-usable forms. Consensus by itself might not completely kill magic, but Consensus (as opposed by the Technocratic Union) PLUS a deficit in magical power in a region could probably do it for at least some kinds of supernatural entities. It's also a sort of setup why magic could be problematic in a number of ways even if a non trivial percentage of humans believe in magic (its still gonna be hard to do much in the way of magic if there's no magical energy to fuel it.)<br /> <br /> One reason I like this view is that it can incorporate a wide range of causative factors without any one necessarily being decisive (even the ones I described). Or if someone wants certain causative factors to be more decisive it can be adjusted in various ways (Even removing ideas you like.) The broad idea is that the environment was intended to be sustainable and for whatever reasons gradually became unsustainable, and the impact of humans on it (again in various ways) made the situation worse. It's the sort of thing that can bring you to some mega-crisis point (the various settings apocalyptic 'endings' - even if you buy into a cyclic view, some 'endings' could be worse than others, with a failing system you could face a single, catastrophic ending.) And since V20 and V5 were a thing I adapted that thinking to the idea that the 'mega-crisis' was survived (or is still ongoing in some form) but it did not prove to be an *end* (at least not yet. It could be a delayed outcome, or perhaps some of the causative factors were mended or altered, or something else came into play.) - the 'cycle' may still be ongoing and things might be terrible but the world didn't come to an end. Things might even have evolved or changed (a possibility left in many of the various 'end game' scearnios which can also play into changes between the original revised and later iterations that got evolved)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25533,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25533-mister_dunpeal","user":"Mister_Dunpeal","date":". 04-24-2022, 07:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25533-mister_dunpeal","name":"Mister_Dunpeal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"308"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-24T17:58:59-07:00","id":1479457,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479445#post1479445\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Granted, how large Would a reality zone created by a mega-church be? I'm guessing it would depend as much on whether it was located closer or farther away from the center of a city as it would it's population.</div> </div> </div> </div>Cities aren't the only concern here. Two competing religious communities in the same isolated area should have it's own distinct impact.<br /> <br /> Also, I would surmise (esp. that most megachurches aren't isolated enough in both geography and population to generate a reality zone) that far more often megachurches result in wellsprings. It would be very likely that the ventures of Primal Utility were at least heavily influenced by the ability of large scale organized religion to result in more predictable wellsprings by getting a bunch of faithful together in the right place, at the right time, to be primed (no pun intended) for the emotional catharsis necessary to result in a sudden release of a large amount of quintessence.<br /> <br /> You'd also have the impact of the underpinnings of things like mythic threads and blatancy that would make magic much much easier, even if never fully growing into a stable reality zone that endures from day-to-day instead of moments of enough emotional power being gathered at once.<br /> <br /> We know such reality zones can be large. The Empty Quarter controlled by the Taftani might be the largest and, at least in geography, is a giant reality zone. But it's also been shrink as the Union has it surrounded and have been chipping away at its edges while the Taftani have realized they can't win the siege and their newest generations have completely shifted methods. A Taftani (one that can be seen as a Revised era pioneer of the M20 default) was also responsible for about a city-block sized reality zone in New York City.<br /> <br /> Most of the examples in the books are pretty small in scale, because they're focused around small (population and geography) communities in isolated areas. They require a concentration of belief that tends to get focused on a sacred ritual spot and then fade as you get away from it as belief gets more diffuse and runs into other influences on consensus.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-24T18:22:17-07:00","id":1479459,"title":"","content":"For what it's worth, the upcoming <b>Lore of the Traditions</b> is going to be introducing an optional rule for \u201cstable wellsprings\u201d that will give the Celestial Chorus a counterpart to the Syndicate's Ventures, but based on communities of worship instead of businesses. I'm sure the concept could be generalized to other groups; <b>The Rich Bastard's Guide to Magick</b> describes one faction (in Africa, I think) that is using communities as Ventures, by the slight bit of sophistry that anything that improves the community counts as \u201cprofit\u201d. Stable Wellsprings could easily encompass that notion as well, and wouldn't even need the sophistry. <br /> <br /> In short, Stable Wellsprings have the potential to serve as the general notion of community activity serving as a source of Primal Energy \u2014 which in turn feeds into my last post about Terranorming: if groups outside the Technocracy were to discover the secret behind Terranorming, they'd find that they already have the necessary tools in place to engage in it themselves.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 08-24-2022, 10:50 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-26T07:52:19-07:00","id":1479546,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479445#post1479445\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't understand your point here? Most people don't believe they're saints that can call on miracles down from on high, true, but that they still believe that certain priests can. I'm not joking. There are massive churches where thousands of people go to witness priests perform miracles they genuinely believe are real.</div> </div> </div> </div>My point was about your claims about Technocractical Paradigm not being believed enough. That is important because such beliefs alter reality too...more on that below<br /> <br /> And on WoD that belief it's kinda true. Mages or sorcerers can call on miracles down from on high...and people also believes on &quot;false&quot; priests that fake it. And that's also true on WoD<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Many a celestial chorus member could stand on that platform and go ham because the entire place would be a reality zone made specifically for their paradigm(IF the consensus was what was stopping them, the fact that they don't implies consensus isn't the main factor). </div> </div> </div>Yeah, and that does happen. There are rules even that suggest a ritual with more than 100 participants *could* be made Coincidental, even on a Zone where it normaly would be Vulgar. On a Zone where it wouldn't, it won't even be necessary.<br /> <br /> But that's not the *only way* the Consensus works. We're dealing with phenomena that go strictly beyond the RAW and into the Lore, so you should consider the posibility of the Consensus affecting more than just what's strictly ruled. Keep this in mind. Keep this on mind, because that's where I want to go.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm lost as to you point here? On one hand, I actually think I kind of disagree, but I'm not sure I'm understanding you correctly. </div> </div> </div>My point it's that people doesn't believe all on the same impossibilities. There are many religions and superstitions on the world, shaping the world, and not all believe on the same things. The guy that believes in aliens scoffs at the guy that believes on demons, and even among the religious of the same religion there's discrepancy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Isn't this exactly what I was saying though? Consensus couldn't be the only factor and I went a step further to say that consensus might not even have been very important in the first place. </div> </div> </div>No, because you went a step further.<br /> <br /> If the Consensus - understood as more than just RZ - couldn't have been an important factor, or wasn't <i>possibly </i>cappable of enacting a change on how Paradox works, then one of the factions of the Ascencion War would be factualy wrong. That's why I find important to talk about this point.<br /> <br /> We are in the Lore: Mage Lore claims The Consensus it's the <i>collective will </i>of the millons of Avatars that exists in the world. That's not just Sleepers, but Mages (and maybe other beings - that's debatable). Sleepers influence the Consensus more than Mages because they are a lot more ... but ultimately, the Consensus it's like a big collection of rituals. It's like if hundreds of mages were working to alter reality<br /> <br /> On that sense, I want to consider a few things:<br /> <br /> *Population increasing was brought on before: With the increase of human population you have *more* Avatars changing reality. That could have changed the kind of &quot;magick&quot; the Consensus it's cappable of making. Among other things, that it's influence could, perhaps, expand further.<br /> <br /> *Consider how the Technocracy pushed for a Global Consensus...didn't that kinda work?<br /> <br /> Think about how Paradox would manifest on Cruzade vs. how it would manifest on the modern era.<br /> <br /> On Cruzade, European Paradox manifests as God ire against the hubris of mages (as demons or angels). A Hermetic suffering Paradox would have a demon coming to get him, and nobody would say &quot;this is impossible&quot;, they would say &quot;the socerer was punished by God for his Hubris&quot;. Ironically, an Infernal mage would have an easier time casting precisely because he's acting in accordance with these beliefs.<br /> <br /> Paradox wasn't the same everywhere...albeit to some extent, it went further than the actual Zones because even on places with lot's of pagans, or Sanctums, it was still the Scourge<br /> <br /> On the moder era, Paradox doesn't manifest in a way so influenced by local belief. It's much more homogeneous, And that can't be explained by just terranorming (non the least because not ALL the world it's terranormed).<br /> <br /> So, Paradox isn't AS influenced by local belief as it used to be. Could be the Weaver ... but why couldn't that be because the Consensus it's a little more Global now, like the Technocracy wanted?. I think it's a mistake to be adamant about the Consensus underlying influence being entirely limited to it's Zones, non the least becuase the concept was invented prior to M20 and it's Zones.<br /> <br /> Religions don't believe all on the same impossibilities. Conflicting beliefs could be pushing against each other. As permy previou argument: There's a very solid consensus about cars working nearly everywhere - but if you go to the India you won't see that much of a Consensus about Jesus being real.<br /> <br /> If Consensus has some global effects, that underlie zones, then those effects would clash with opposing effects the moment one belief or religion clashes with another. And it could be having subtler effects than just defining Vulgarity.<br /> <br /> *Because the Consensus *is* <b>Humanity </b>doing magick. What about <i>Terranorming</i>?. I would say the new data could be very much coherent with the OOC belief of those that believe the Consensus is important: <i>What are Ventures, if not something that's defined as</i> &quot;<b>Valuable</b> <b>for Humanity</b>&quot;<i>?, <b>How does one define Value</b> if not trough a Paradigm/Belief</i>? The fact that &quot;sophistry&quot; of the Africans can alter the value of Ventures, it means that beliefs definitely have something to do with the flow of Quintessence ... if the Consensus it's the sum of belief, and beliefs about Value can allow one to produce or tap on Quintessence, and then <i>we posit that such are part and parcel to change the underpining Foundations of reality</i> ... <i>then, why be so adamant that the Consensus can't change those</i>?<br /> <br /> SO, I wouldn't categorically say that the Consensus isn't important. If the fundamental aspect of terranorming can come from humanity activities, and mages can use it to terranorm within belief ... I would say so can humanity's collective effort, taken not as <i>Zoning </i>but as a potential far-reaching ritual that includes magely effort - if perhaps it was done somewhat by accident, somewhat by the Technocracy pushing the rigth buttons.<br /> <br /> It's ST's discretion - look at the Consensus importance as a &quot;dial&quot; you can increase or decrease. If it's too low, the Traditions are factualy wrong about their beliefs, thus can never win unless they shift those (and the Technocracy will fail at many of it's goals - they want to be immortal too), and the bigger part of Mage canon it's wrong. If it's too high, then the Technocracy it's factualy wrong, it's preventing humanity's potential, and it's also a disservice to Crossover and sometimes could clash with RL logic (and could be hard or distastefull to correct it).<br /> <br /> In general, my stance it's to push against both.<br /> <br /> I do believe that with immortality there is more than just the Consensus going on. But I wouldn't just discard the Consensus as a factor <i>strengthening </i>reality, or a factor that could potentialy make it softer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-26-2022, 11:14 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-26T09:12:14-07:00","id":1479550,"title":"","content":"Yep. Just to clarify: I'm not arguing that the Consensus doesn't matter, or that the Consensus can't change Reality Zones. The whole reason I came up with the idea of \u201csoft\u201d and \u201chard\u201d RZs was to facilitate the Storyteller being able to fine-tune the relationship he wants the Consensus to have with reality \u2014 possibly even handling it differently in different parts of the same setting. Want to emphasize the role that the Consensus has in shaping reality? Feature a \u201csoft\u201d RZ where everything must conform to the Consensus. Want to emphasize that we have to deal with reality as it is, instead of how we wish it would be? Feature a \u201chard\u201d RZ where the Consensus has a comparatively small impact. You might even frame the Ascension War itself at least partially around this notion, with the Technocracy mostly favoring the hardening of RZs (after shaping them to their liking, of course) while the Traditions prefer to \u201csoften\u201d RZs. <br /> <br /> Nor am I saying that there can't be global influences where the Consensus is concerned \u2014 though I see that as more of an ideal being worked toward rather than an established fact. And ironically, the group that has done the most to promote a global Consensus isn't the Technocracy, but a Tradition: the Virtual Adepts, who pioneered the Internet and broke down the barriers of space that used to isolate RZs from each other. But despite RZs not officially being a thing prior to M20, I would say that they've always being implicitly there. <br /> <br /> As for the nature of the Consensus: I agree with the notion that it's the collective result of Avatar-empowered beliefs \u2014 meaning that humans contribute to it while dogs and cats don't; but also meaning that individual Sleepers don't influence reality nearly as much as individual Awakened do. I generally <i>don't</i> consider the Awakened to be a significant part of the Consensus, because their ability to alter reality is mostly represented by the Magick rules; and when they're <i>not</i> willworking in a way that's best handled through said rules, they're no more influential than a Sleeper. In short, I tend to view the Consensus as the influence of human Avatars, ignoring the deliberate activities of the Awakened. Otherwise, a mage could never \u201cgo against the Consensus\u201d because the \u201cConsensus\u201d would be whatever he's trying to <i>do</i>. <br /> <br /> (More thoughts later; I've got some IRL issues to deal with right now.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-26T10:04:07-07:00","id":1479552,"title":"","content":"Quick note: the population in China in 2015 was about 1.38 billion; the population of India was 1.31 billion. (The United States came in third at 0.32 billion.) Together, China and India make up approximately 36% of the world population. Africa makes up another 17% of the world's population, followed by Europe with 10%, Latin America and the Caribbean at 8%, the US and Canada at 5%, and Oceana at 0.5%. To the extent that there's a \u201cglobal Consensus\u201d, it <i>ought</i> to be dominated by the beliefs prevalent in China, India, and Africa. <br /> <br /> It is for this reason, among others, that I prefer to say that \u201clocal reality\u201d tends to take precedence over \u201cglobal reality\u201d where that's a reasonable option, and that \u201cglobal reality\u201d tends to lean toward the \u201chard\u201d end of the scale, with \u201cglobal truths\u201d (such as it <i>being</i> a globe) generally not being subject to the \u201cvotes\u201d of the Consensus.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-26T12:52:02-07:00","id":1479557,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479552#post1479552\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To the extent that there's a \u201cglobal Consensus\u201d, it <i>ought</i> to be dominated by the beliefs prevalent in China, India, and Africa.</div> </div> </div> </div>Why would that be a problem?<br /> <br /> There are many conflicting faiths on China. Taoists believe in immortals, but Buddhists believe on impermanence - and they're the majoritary faith. Everyone believes on cars. Of course, this is simplifying these beliefs a lot - but you get my point: There's not much of an &quot;Oriental Consensus&quot; that could clash with our real world and should be shaping it onto something wierd.<br /> <br /> Really, &quot;our world&quot; as a Consensus it's almost a truism. As far as the Global Consensus can exist, you could always say that even a whopping 36% of agreement won't be enough (albeit that's not necessary) to make a strong global shift, you can be guaranteed that people who believes &quot;things fall down&quot; it's going to be larger than that (flatearthers believe the earth moves up instead - but for this purpose it's the same :P ), same with planes and other everyday city-stuff.<br /> <br /> Where this falls down, it's with the details. I think, precisely, questions about localized places. Or how all of this come to be, or - more importantly - how it could change: How Technocrats &quot;introduce&quot; new stuff, for instance. Obviously it's not by convincing 36% of the population at once!. This is, I think, where the Awakened as &quot;spearheads of change&quot; shine best, and the global needs to take a back seat to the local.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I generally <i>don't</i> consider the Awakened to be a significant part of the Consensus, because their ability to alter reality is mostly represented by the Magick rules; and when they're <i>not</i> willworking in a way that's best handled through said rules, they're no more influential than a Sleeper. In short, I tend to view the Consensus as the influence of human Avatars, ignoring the deliberate activities of the Awakened. Otherwise, a mage could never \u201cgo against the Consensus\u201d because the \u201cConsensus\u201d would be whatever he's trying to <i>do</i>. </div> </div> </div>In general there's no need. Albeit we know it happens.I do think that the ability to shape reality it's related to willworking, but I want to take <i>willworking</i> as something of a broader context than just casting a single Rote.<br /> <br /> \u200bFor instance: We know Sanctums are RZ, with a &quot;Consensus&quot; of one person's belief. We know there's no single &quot;Create Sanctum&quot; Rote (albeit I think there could be ways to fasten the process), but that this happens on the course of casting a lot of effects on a place overtime. Even if none of the effects were intended to &quot;form&quot; a Sanctum, it's implied that this will happen overtime.<br /> <br /> Mages casting spells, clashing their wills, &quot;represing&quot; places to terranorm, etc...could've had a profounder effect on the world than what the actual willworkers intended. As per what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> was saying.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-26-2022, 04:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-28T09:11:54-07:00","id":1479654,"title":"","content":"The WOD seems based on Tolkien-like ideas about the world becoming more mundane over time, and Neverending Story ideas about the slow death of imagination. They came up with the whole consensus thing in an attempt to justify it, but it never really fit as an explanation. If the world is based on consensus, then we'd have never left the world of caveman folklore, and placating spirits would be as mundane a skill as engineering and medicine in the real world. The original writers seemed to work backwards from their conclusion, first deciding that their universe was a place of increasing mundanity, then deciding that it was science's fault, which then lead to scientists being The Man. It's the sort of chain of thought that makes sense when you are writing fantasy like LOTR, but which doesn't hold up to fridge-logic. Especially since the most tyrannical forces in our world are very much against the scientific method.<br /> <br /> Ultimately the WOD is very meta. Science didn't destroy magick, but it did weaken magical fantasy stories. You don't see a lot of fiction these days things are as wild as they were in the oldest folklore. Even Harry Potter type stories are nowhere near as crazy as the oldest folklore myths.<br /> <br /> That's the thing about the WOD's design: on some level it never forgets that these are fantasy stories, and that it's telling a story about how fantasy stories have become more mundane over time, and barely pretending that this is about a universe where reality has become more mundane over time. Much of the WOD only works in this very meta/allegorical way.<br /> <br /> So effectively, those old wizards were weakened by something akin to The Nothing from the Neverending Story, because those wizards were just fantasy characters, and changing culture in the real world made their particular set of fantasy tropes change.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-28T11:16:18-07:00","id":1479660,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CajunKhan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479654#post1479654\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If the world is based on consensus, then we'd have never left the world of caveman folklore, and placating spirits would be as mundane a skill as engineering and medicine in the real world.</div> </div> </div> </div>This just... really comes off as not getting what the concept of consensus really is.<br /> <br /> The wold is based on a subjective reality. Consensus is the result of mages trying to manipulate human perceptions to benefit some mages over others (aka the Ascension War). Before mages bothered with that, consensus wasn't a thing (even if subjectivity in reality was a thing).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The original writers seemed to work backwards from their conclusion,... </div> </div> </div>Most writers do. Are you claiming you started this post without your conclusion decided and just starting free typing out until your reached a conclusion?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Especially since the most tyrannical forces in our world are very much against the scientific method. </div> </div> </div>The Technocracy, as an organization, doesn't actually care about the real scientific method. They care about it only as a tool that enables them to convince humanity it came up with stuff the Technocracy feeds them. The very fact that the Technocracy has a Time Table for human scientific and technological advancement is proof enough that actual science isn't what interests them. Like most of those tyrants you're referencing, the Union as a long history of purging true scientists from their ranks when they get inconvenient. Sorry. When they stop being &quot;mutual.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You don't see a lot of fiction these days things are as wild as they were in the oldest folklore. </div> </div> </div>No offense... but I think you really need to broaden your horizons. There are lots of wild and crazy stories out in pop culture that makes old mythology tame. The only real dissonance is changing moral standards. Older stories can often shock with what's seen as good and proper values, even if the events are far less wild.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-29T05:53:01-07:00","id":1479688,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage#post1479349\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One of the things that I found while browsing the internet was that a large portion of the world Still believes in magic in some form. At least 30% of americans believe in astrology, 41% believe n psychic powers, 42% believe in some form os new-age spiritual energy(not counting astrology or psychic powers), 85% of the human race is religious, Ireland Still doesn't pave over fairy circles because old beliefs are held by the majority even in public office, etc. And that is all just Modern Belief in the IRL version of earth, which is suppose to be orders of magnitude less superstitious and well adjusted than people in the WOD<br /> <br /> Why was it that in the setting of DA Mage, when superstition and spirituality where still at their Peaks, that magic started &quot;dying&quot;. Why did mages start losing their access to easy immortality? Why did the Scourge/Paradox start raising it's ugly head?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> 85% of human race might be religious, but it doesn't mean that 85% of the human paradigm is &quot;God is real and science and technology and reason don't work at all&quot;<br /> My personal headcanon is that due to the very distinctive degrees of religious beliefs and the fact that such thing doesn't exclude believing in reason and science by itself, then the reason paradigm is anyway very strong in modern day world<br /> While the fact that 41% of americans believe in psychic powers (if that's true) is why magick still exists in modern day world...<br /> <br /> What I'm saying is that IRL world's Modern Belief isn't all that magical even if taking into consideration the data you brought up. Religion doesn't exclude the belief in an ordered world.<br /> Many times it is a matter of &quot;ordered indeed, but from above&quot; than anything else.<br /> <br /> Still, I understand your point. It's kinda strange that magic started &quot;dying&quot; in XII century and not, let's say, in the XV<br /> I can headcanon it with my own explanation of religion as &quot;ordered, but from above&quot; but it's still a bit odd due to the reasons you listed.<br /> <br /> Unless we think as mass monotheistic religions as a way to shift the paradigm to a more collective kind of paradigm in place of personal ones<br /> But it's at odds with the fact that one of the Traditions is the religious faction","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Ever since the Followers of Set rebranded themselves as The Ministry, I can barely keep a straight face around them.&quot; - Ramona","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/15944-ravnos","name":"Ravnos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar15944_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-29T09:04:28-07:00","id":1479702,"title":"","content":"Don't forget that one of the founding Conventions was the Cabal of Pure Thought; it wasn't until the 19th century (Victorian Mage) that the Technocracy went secular.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-29T09:54:01-07:00","id":1479710,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ravnos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479688#post1479688\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">85% of human race might be religious, but it doesn't mean that 85% of the human paradigm is &quot;God is real and science and technology and reason don't work at all&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>That isn't my point. Belief in magic and belief in technology aren't mutually exclusive.<br /> <br /> I found that disbelief in magic isn't as prevalent as the mage setting assumes, so I was looking for alternate explanations for what causes paradox.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ravnos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479688#post1479688\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While the fact that 41% of americans believe in psychic powers (if that's true) is why magick still exists in modern day world...</div> </div> </div> </div>Would a 100% disbelief in something by humanity make awakened magic of specific paradigms completely impossible?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ravnos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479688#post1479688\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What I'm saying is that IRL world's Modern Belief isn't all that magical even if taking into consideration the data you brought up. Religion doesn't exclude the belief in an ordered world.<br /> Many times it is a matter of &quot;ordered indeed, but from above&quot; than anything else.<br /> <br /> Still, I understand your point. It's kinda strange that magic started &quot;dying&quot; in XII century and not, let's say, in the XV<br /> I can headcanon it with my own explanation of religion as &quot;ordered, but from above&quot; but it's still a bit odd due to the reasons you listed.<br /> <br /> Unless we think as mass monotheistic religions as a way to shift the paradigm to a more collective kind of paradigm in place of personal ones<br /> But it's at odds with the fact that one of the Traditions is the religious faction</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":6383,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","user":"Prometheas","date":"04-30-2022, 02:24 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-29T12:30:51-07:00","id":1479730,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479710#post1479710\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That isn't my point. Belief in technology and belief in technology aren't mutually exclusive. <br /> <br /> I found that disbelief in magic isn't as prevalent as the mage setting assumes, so I was looking for alternate explanations for what causes paradox.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's literally what Earthly Foundations (M20 pp.612\u2013613) are for: they define certain things that are Vulgar \u201c<span style=\"font-family:times new Roman\">no matter <i>who's</i> doing them, <i>where</i> they're doing them, or <i>how</i> they're being done. In game terms, acts that violate these Earthly Foundations contradict the baseline reality for Earth.</span>\u201d What I've been saying on the matter has involved coming up with some better ideas as to just what the Earthly Foundations <i>are</i>, and to provide in-game metaphysical means for <i>softening</i> them and bringing some dynamism to something that, at least as presented in the core book, is very, very static. <br /> <br /> In the book, Earthly Foundations are what they are; and there's no indication that they can ever change. I've been suggesting that they <i>can</i> change; but in order to <i>be</i> Earthly Foundations, the things that can change them cannot be the things that shift Reality Zones: again, M20 indicates that you can shift RZs and provides guidelines on how to do it; but it implies that EFs are an exception: if you could shift an EF simply by altering the Consensus, they wouldn't be defining things as Vulgar regardless of who, where, or how. <br /> <br /> It's quite possible that even saying that Terranorming can shift EFs is going too far, and that the only things that can shift EFs are archmagi and other godlike entities. Even the Technocracy's massive success in spreading Technocratic Reality around the globe isn't officially presented as them affecting the EFs in any way; at most, it can be read as them aligning their Enlightened Science with the EFs. I'm not personally a fan of being that strict; but it <i>is</i> a valid reading of M20; and to the extent that there <i>is</i> a \u201ccorrect\u201d reading of it, I think that that's it. <br /> <br /> It's <i>because</i> I'm not a fan of being that strict that I've been thinking about options such as there being ways to soften or harden them, with the hardness \u201csetting\u201d determining how much of a given RZ's nature is defined by the EFs vs how much is defined by the Consensus. If you don't buy that the Consensus can give rise to Paradox (because there isn't sufficient opposition to magic in the Consensus to justify that), then you'll want the hardness turned up so that the EFs can be the source of Paradox instead. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479710#post1479710\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Would a 100% disbelief in something by humanity make awakened magic of specific paradigms completely impossible?</div> </div> </div> </div>No. The only thing that can make Awakened magic impossible is deliberate interference by other supernatural powers (usually Awakened Magick; but anyone who can perform countermagic will do), or <i>some</i> aspects of Earthly Foundations \u2014 though that's very rare, and tends to be more about such things as \u201cthe process of how magic works\u201d*. Sleeper Avatars are weak enough that even when aggregated, they're not powerful enough to block an Arete 1 mage; they can only attach consequences to his actions. <br /> <br /> There are some things that even the combined might of billions of Sleepers worldwide <i>can't do</i>, because it's not <i>just</i> a matter of sheer force. Awakened Magick isn't just a matter of Will; it's a matter of <i>Enlightened</i> Will. That Enlightenment (or Arete, in the case of non-technos) isn't about raw power alone. For raw power, I'd rather look at the Avatar Background. Rather, Arete/Enlightenment is more like \u201cfinesse\u201d: more like Dexterity than Strength, to give the physical attributes as an analogy. And a billion klutzes are no more agile than one klutz. <br /> <br /> So a billion Sleepers can punish you when you use Magick, but they can't prevent you from using Magick. By contrast, <i>one</i> Awakened adversary can prevent you from using Magick. <br /> <br /> <hr /><br /> <span style=\"color:gray\">* A mage cannot cast a spell that alters what kind of Feat a given working counts as; nor can she cast a spell that causes a ritual to be performed as a Rite when it would normally bea Great Work (M20 pp.541\u2013542). You cannot alter the way Acting in Concert works (M20 pp.542\u2013543). <br /> <br /> In short, the <i>rules of magic</i> are not something that can be altered by magic. I mention this because the original question was about <i>just this sort of change</i>: alterations to the way Paradox works. It doesn't mean that the rules of magic can't change; they obviously can, because they <i>have</i>. But <i>that</i> kind of change isn't even something that the <i>Awakened</i> are capable of (except <i>maybe</i> in the aggregate, and over a long period of time, much like Sleepers are only capable of some of the things that the Awakened can do in the aggregate), let alone the <i>Consensus</i>.</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 04-30-2022, 02:45 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-30T00:06:00-07:00","id":1479790,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CajunKhan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479654#post1479654\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The WOD seems based on Tolkien-like ideas about the world becoming more mundane over time, and Neverending Story ideas about the slow death of imagination.</div> </div> </div> </div>While I don't think you got exactly how the Consensus works on Mage, <b>this</b> is a very relevant point.<br /> <br /> Not only for Mage, by the way, but for the entire WoD.<br /> <br /> Old vampires were veritable gods while as time goes on we reach the Time of Thin Blood. Corruption is the enemy, but the true malady of the world is the ever-encroaching Weaver. Glamour becomes scarce as Banality takes hold of the world, bringing the approaching Winter. Magic is dying under the weight of convention.<br /> <br /> The whole setting is built over the premise that the world is moving from an epic past to a mediocre future, with a strong sense of predictability that is equated to apathy and uniformity. And the recurrent theme of approaching Judgement, present since the beginning even if not as urgent as in Revised, is strictly linked to this. It was all part of the End of History zeitgeist of the 90s. And yes, at the end of the day, many, many aspects of the setting were built in reverse to generate/justify this feeling and an urgency to fight mundanity with wonder.<br /> <br /> But those notions are wrong, as many the writers originally had. What we can do now is recognize the flawed foundation for the lore and change it, since this is fiction and we can do such things.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ravnos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479688#post1479688\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What I'm saying is that IRL world's Modern Belief isn't all that magical even if taking into consideration the data you brought up. Religion doesn't exclude the belief in an ordered world.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ancient Belief wasn't, either, that's a common misconception, but a misconception.<br /> <br /> People had distinct beliefs, but the same expectation of an ordered and predictable reality. Myths, gods and spirits were just explanations for this order. Our ancestors built mathematically significant temples and giant calendars in stone because they were secure in the fact that the laws of nature would remain in place. Life for an average farmer two thousands years ago was just as mundane as it is today, ghost stories and weird talismans were basically as present, and even by the writings of scholars from ancient times we know they were as frustrated with superstitions as modern scientists.<br /> <br /> The setting <b>is</b> built under the assumption of the world becoming more &quot;rational&quot;, but this assumption is objectively wrong.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-30T09:15:19-07:00","id":1479815,"title":"","content":"Having gotten my Masters in History for examining the link between Calvinism and the Enlightenment choosing the Republic as the preferred governmental form (preferred not required), I found out that most pre-Enlightenment Western societies believed the Earth was decaying. The also believed that each generation of human beings was less in all ways than the preceding generations. The Calvinists rejected this belief as rejecting the goodness of God. The Enlightenment secularized this belief (as they did with many aspects of Calvin's thought). <br /> <br /> The Romantics, in rejecting the Enlightenment (mainly because of the horrors of the French Revolution and Napoleon's wars) also embraced the view of the world as decaying. The fading of magic and wonder became a standard trope among the Romantics. From there it became a trope so standard in fantasy fiction that it was nearly unconscious. Just think how rare before the turn of the century Magic in the distant future was. Other than Ashton Smith and the animated film &quot;Wizards&quot; examples are thin on the ground. <br /> <br /> White Wolf simply followed an ancient trope.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-30T13:19:54-07:00","id":1479828,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479815#post1479815\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just think how rare before the turn of the century Magic in the distant future was. Other than Ashton Smith and the animated film &quot;Wizards&quot; examples are thin on the ground.<br /> <br /> White Wolf simply followed an ancient trope.</div> </div> </div> </div>Pretty much that. A simple example is the Greek notion of &quot;human ages&quot;, with Gold being followed by Silver, Bronze then Iron, but with a twist that &quot;superior&quot; humans aren't necessarily as good at morals or this &quot;society&quot; thing.<br /> <br /> But this old trope has variations based on the times, and the form it took in the 90s was very particular, if indeed still owning much to the past of the trope.<br /> <br /> Even because in the 70s and 80s this notion of magic-less future wasn't so strong. At the time it was common to see the &quot;reversion&quot; of the trope, a post-apocalyptic near-apocalyptic future where fantasy and magic resurfaced among the ruins of our society, as in the Dying Earth series that inspired D&amp;D.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-30T14:39:38-07:00","id":1479839,"title":"","content":"Like Thundarr The Barbarian, you mean?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-30T17:03:38-07:00","id":1479846,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CajunKhan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479839#post1479839\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Like Thundarr The Barbarian, you mean?</div> </div> </div> </div>Precisely.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-01T01:32:39-07:00","id":1479873,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ravnos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479688#post1479688\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While the fact that 41% of americans believe in psychic powers (if that's true) is why magick still exists in modern day world...</div> </div> </div> </div>On this point, I would argue the continuing existence has as much, if not more, to do with humans just having a seemingly inborn tendency towards <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magical_thinking\" target=\"_blank\">Magical Thinking</a>.<br /> <br /> It's why perfectly secular and scientifically rational people will still smack a malfunctioning machine and tell it to work properly. As if this object of metal, plastic, and wires was a stubborn beast of burden, incompetent servant, or misbehaving child. Humans just project agency on things. Anthropomorphizing the inanimate and insensate, even if, by all rights, they should &quot;know better&quot;.<br /> <br /> Even in these days of scientific reason, people will still default to a kind of Animism.<br /> <br /> People continue believing in jinxes - that speaking of something will cause it to happen. They believe in strings of bad luck, which in truth are little more than confirmation bias. They repeat mantras like &quot;Make America Great Again&quot; or &quot;Vote Blue No Matter Who&quot;, as if doing so enough times will manifest that into reality (and that if it didn't, it's because they, or more likely their designated scapegoats, failed to follow the mantra hard enough). People don't like to live in murder houses, even when they don't believe in ghosts or know the murderer is safely behind bars, if for no other reason than unspoken &quot;bad vibes&quot;. And every adult disbelieves in monsters hiding in the dark, up until the lights go out.<br /> <br /> No matter how well informed or how much they internalize science, quirks of human psychology come to the fore that are of a more magical nature. It's not merely enough to &quot;know better&quot; about the facts concerning the world. For there is always more ignorance, more of something we don't, as individuals, understand. We must come to understand how our minds work - the cognitive pitfalls in our psychology - before we can actually catch ourselves succumbing to magical thinking. Even then, it still happens.<br /> <br /> This, I think, is where the lingering effects of Magic are in the Mage setting. Call these psychological forces Earthly Foundations local to the human species. It means humans, even at our most &quot;rational&quot;, will continue grandfathering in mysticism.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":6156,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","user":"Bluecho","date":". 05-01-2022, 04:34 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-01T03:30:49-07:00","id":1479879,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CajunKhan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479839#post1479839\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Like Thundarr The Barbarian, you mean?</div> </div> </div> </div>In many ways the show copied both Clark Ashton Smith's &quot;Zotique,&quot; and Ralph Bakshi's &quot;Wizards.&quot; Although I always thought &quot;Thundarr&quot; was more fun than &quot;Wizards.&quot; <br /> <br /> By the way, the Magic in the &quot;Dying Earth&quot; books by Jack Vance was, like Lovecraft's magic, actually supposed to be an exotic science. The difference was that in Vance's setting human beings set up the magic. It was like a set of special effects that people could call upon. That's why the spells were limited in scope, they were never spells they were subroutines.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":857,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","user":"Astromancer","date":". 05-01-2022, 06:36 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-04T17:19:28-07:00","id":1480136,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1479873#post1479873\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's why perfectly secular and scientifically rational people will still smack a malfunctioning machine and tell it to work properly. As if this object of metal, plastic, and wires was a stubborn beast of burden, incompetent servant, or misbehaving child. Humans just project agency on things. Anthropomorphizing the inanimate and insensate, even if, by all rights, they should &quot;know better&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's also the very real possibility that a good jolt will move loose connections, wiring, and small mechanical parts back into position enough to make a machine start functioning again. My dad was a TV repairman, and while his first step was to make sure a faulty TV set was plugged in and turned on, the second was to give it a good thump on the side and see if that fixed whatever the problem was. The talking may be superstition or an affectation, but the smacking often gets results.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-04T18:28:03-07:00","id":1480137,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matt the Bruins fan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480136#post1480136\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's also the very real possibility that a good jolt will move loose connections, wiring, and small mechanical parts back into position enough to make a machine start functioning again. My dad was a TV repairman, and while his first step was to make sure a faulty TV set was plugged in and turned on, the second was to give it a good thump on the side and see if that fixed whatever the problem was. The talking may be superstition or an affectation, but the smacking often gets results.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yet it is overdone, specially nowadays. The smacking gets results sometimes, not often.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-04T19:33:26-07:00","id":1480141,"title":"","content":"It's also still done despite changes in technology. A VCR needs empty space and more robust parts to handle the cassette mechanisms. CRT monitors also need empty space for their projectors and fairly durable parts to handle the constant repetitive motion of aiming the tube properly. A smack might help, or at least find, a problem without causing more.<br /> <br /> As things like flat screen TVs that have wireless connectivity to stream things off the Internet have gotten more dominant in the consumer market, the manufactures keep trying to get repair techs to stop this behavior because they're tightly packed with electronics that aren't meant to move around. It's way more likely you make things worse with a modern TV by hitting it than get lucky and knock a part back into place.<br /> <br /> Yet the behavior continues regardless of the reality of when it may be a good idea or not.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-05T02:56:02-07:00","id":1480156,"title":"","content":"Setting aside using old &quot;wisdom&quot; in new and different contexts, to which it's not appropriate, my point had nothing to do with the smacking of electronics.<br /> <br /> My point was with demanding the electronics work, often verbally as well as physically.<br /> <br /> The issue is with the projection of <i>agency</i> onto an object that has none. A fact we, on an intellectual level, know full well. We do it anyway. THAT is Magical Thinking in action.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-05T04:11:34-07:00","id":1480157,"title":"","content":"That... isn't really what magical thinking means as an academic concept. Magical thinking is, most broadly, assigning a casual results to non-correlated actions. Or to put it another way, believing that one thing you did caused something else to happen, despite no (internal) logical connection between them. The way many pop-culture uses of &quot;karma&quot; are magical thinking: that something good/bad happened to someone because they did something previously that was good/bad (the actual religious concept of karma is a lot more complicated). Thinking that your boss bumped into a coworker and got hot coffee all over themselves happened because they just assigned you mandatory overtime despite knowing you had important personal business to see to is magical thinking (unless you have good reason to believe said coworker did that on purpose). Your 4th paragraph in post #35 had better examples of magical thinking (strings of bad luck are a good one),<br /> <br /> What you're talking about is anthropomorphism: where humans assign human features to non-human objects. Such as treating our tools as if they understand us, and can follow our directions/demands/pleas/threats/etc. even though we intellectually know they can't. <br /> <br /> Both mental activities probably have similar neurological roots (our brains are not built for rational processing, and most irrational-but-common behaviors can be linked to so form of our brains trying to develop an energy efficient way to apply what our brains are good at doing to approximate rational behavior, or to de-stress after too much energy spent on actual rational behavior. The idea that we can actually become Vulcans and stop doing these things is nonsense. All we can do is budget our emotional energy so we can recognize and avoid them when it is important to do so.<br /> <br /> You're also equivocating a lot between the concept of magical thinking using the word magic, and mysticism.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-05T08:07:42-07:00","id":1480162,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480157#post1480157\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You're also equivocating a lot between the concept of magical thinking using the word magic, and mysticism.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>It may be the wrong term, but I feel the point is still the same.<br /> <br /> Assigning object a level of Anthropomorphism, Magical thinking, general superstition, etc. It all contributes to the fact that people generally develop non-rational, non-scientific behaviors even if they're given the best education.<br /> <br /> I believe the point was that People just generally believe in magic on some level, even if not in the same way or same extent.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-05T09:22:20-07:00","id":1480172,"title":"","content":"Paradox it's described on the books as, rougly, the reaction that it's caused when the Mage's Will clashes against Reality(tm). Just like the mage changes Reality, Reality changes the mage, when Reality *breaks*, it hurts the mage - That is Paradox<br /> <br /> The Consensus enters in the equation as a way to shape the rules of Reality (on Earth) in ways that are beneficial to some mages and perjudicial to others. *How much* the Consensus, or belief in general, shapes Reality it's ST discretion.<br /> <br /> Alternate things that could change Reality - at least on a limited fashion - are, of course, more than just possible, but a fact.<br /> <br /> We know high level Spirits do this because they can define the rules of their Private Realms. And so can Mages when they set or alter the rules of their own Realms or Formatted Sectors of the digi-web. In the Horizon Realms, in particular, the creator's domain it's so strong that some rules suggested magick may not generate Paradox even on a Botch.<br /> <br /> Note that you can suffer Paradox - and even more severe forms of it - on Umbral Realms even if there's no other human being in there, but the wizard.<br /> <br /> So: Who sets the rules for Earth?, Who can change them?. It's ST discretion<br /> <br /> If we <i>want </i>the forces <i>behind</i> the Consensus to be what caused Immortality and other spells to become harder,<b> I don't think that *has* to be related to an increase in &quot;rationality&quot; at all</b>. If humanity's collective power to, so to speak, &quot;punish mages&quot; increased overtime - as it did - it would make every bit of sense that the cappability of mages to act &quot;against the norm&quot; would diminish, <i>precisely </i>because humanity was always thinking like that, in every era and every place. And that way of thinking - magical or otherwise - isn't conducent to *every* effect being equally possible<br /> <br /> If we <i>don't want </i>to assume the power belief has over relity in general and magick in particular. Just pick your favorite &quot;A Wizard Did It&quot; Heres a couple:<br /> <br /> *It was the Weaver making reality more Static.<br /> *It was Banality encroaching over the world.<br /> *It was God, tired of mage shenanigans.<br /> *It was a mage that Ascended and did that to Reality as per that old suggestion that ascended mages can affect all Reality at once (that was latter rescued for Awakening)<br /> *It was a Mummy. While higly unfeasible, they do have a Hekau that can do that, if a mummy had a way to beat the Willpower of every magick practicioner in the world.<br /> *It was a Prime 10 Effect.<br /> *It was the increasing in human population, and human Avatar activity, demanding more Quintessence than what the world can give, causing Reality to become more frail (so, the Consensus but more malthusian. Euthanatos kinda believed this once, I think) <hr /><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">A mage cannot cast a spell that alters what kind of Feat a given working counts as; nor can she cast a spell that causes a ritual to be performed as a Rite when it would normally bea Great Work (M20 pp.541\u2013542). You cannot alter the way Acting in Concert works (M20 pp.542\u2013543).<br /> <br /> In short, the <i>rules of magic</i> are not something that can be altered by magic. I mention this because the original question was about <i>just this sort of change</i>: alterations to the way Paradox works. It doesn't mean that the rules of magic can't change; they obviously can, because they <i>have</i>. But <i>that</i> kind of change isn't even something that the <i>Awakened</i> are capable of (except <i>maybe</i> in the aggregate, and over a long period of time, much like Sleepers are only capable of some of the things that the Awakened can do in the aggregate), let alone the <i>Consensus</i></span><span style=\"color:#808080\">.</span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Let's not conflate RAW rules with the Lore about &quot;the rules&quot;. In Mage these get mixed a lot, but there's still a Doylist and a Watsonian perspective that is very important to avoid confusion.<br /> <br /> The Lore says rules changed, but the rules that the books use have a lot more to do with Doylist convenience than with the Lore. That's why you can find ancient mages with Spheres. M20 has no Pillars, and Phill stated very clearly that &quot;Mage it's Spheres&quot; - so you could very well say that Pillars were never a thing, and would still be working within M20 RAW. Or with 1e RAW, or with a lot of 2e RAW.<br /> <br /> And thank God - otherwise one would have to invent a new RPG for every major change mage society had in the Lore, when playing in old eras - and perhaps even a new system for &quot;special characters&quot; that have different beliefs (like how the new Black Hand book declares the Pillar system can still be used with some changes, if you didn't &quot;got the memo&quot; that magick has changed)<br /> <br /> That the rules say magick can't change the rules, doesn't mean so much that magick can't change the rules <i>et all </i>as that the player characters cannot use magick to that end. Because otherwise the game would be unplayable. If magick <i>can </i>do that, it has to be on a <i>storytelling level</i> (like, say, winning the Acencion War).<br /> <br /> The book doesn't say that the difference between a short ritual and a Great Work are anything like Earthly Foundations - in fact they cannot be because otherwise going against them on a limited fashion should just cause Paradox rather than being impossible (like how you *can* float against gravity). The book doesn't equate those rules to *any* sort of in-game construct that was made to interact with the players IOC, wheter solid or soft.<br /> <br /> In general that's the case. The big exception it's the Spheres, and the book recommends players to never talk about them IOC. And I think it was and is a mistake, that only served to cause a lot of confusion, to conflate these two levels.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-05-2022, 01:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-05T12:09:16-07:00","id":1480190,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480162#post1480162\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It may be the wrong term, but I feel the point is still the same.</div> </div> </div> </div>To an extent. The fact that human brains are not rational machines and thus irrational beliefs/behaviors continue is fundamentally true. But that's not quite enough alone to square with the idea that is enough to explain why Magick! still exists in the setting as it does. It might be part of the underlying explanation, but not complete... esp. as it's an extremely modern view on how the mind functions.<br /> <br /> It doesn't mean everyone believes in magic on some level (hence it's equivocating via the academic concept using the word magic even though it's not talking about actual structured mystic practices), it means that human brains are wired in a way that doesn't preclude holding both scientific and mystic beliefs at the same time (proof enough our brains are not rational machines!). There are more steps needed to make the conclusion sound.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It all contributes to the fact that people generally develop non-rational, non-scientific behaviors even if they're given the best education. </div> </div> </div>Because this is factually backwards. We develop non-rational and non-scientific behaviors as soon as we have a functional enough brain to do so. There is no &quot;rationality&quot; or &quot;science&quot; part of our brain. Rational and scientific thinking are trained skills.<br /> <br /> The &quot;best&quot; education isn't about stopping you from forming those beliefs and behaviors; that's impossible as that process is hardwired into our neurological systems. It's about learning how to recognize them for what they are, and then apply yourself to act on more coherent principles. <br /> <br /> I would argue that the truly best education is one that teaches you how to harness all that brain power by maximal cooperation between our non-rational pattern based processing and trained rational logic, rather than pitting them against each other.<br /> <br /> Modern &quot;educated&quot; people tend to see superstition as bad. But in reality superstition is just a technology for altering our neural functions. Like any technology it can be used for good or ill, and can be used thoughtfully or not for either end. Well channeled superstition is as powerful of a technological tool as well crafted weapons in conflict. Superstitious athletes outperform non-superstitious athletes by a significant margin: because superstition is regularly used by athletes to unlock neural pathways reserved for physical expenditures during life or death situations, and then apply those to sports (decidedly not life or death most of the time). Obviously they're not consciously thinking about it that way (and consciously thinking about it can undermine the whole process), but we know it works, and knowing that it works people like coaches and owners heavily encourage it.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-05T12:27:08-07:00","id":1480194,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480190#post1480190\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To an extent. The fact that human brains are not rational machines and thus irrational beliefs/behaviors continue is fundamentally true. But that's not quite enough alone to square with the idea that is enough to explain why Magick! still exists in the setting as it does. It might be part of the underlying explanation, but not complete... esp. as it's an extremely modern view on how the mind functions.<br /> <br /> It doesn't mean everyone believes in magic on some level (hence it's equivocating via the academic concept using the word magic even though it's not talking about actual structured mystic practices), it means that human brains are wired in a way that doesn't preclude holding both scientific and mystic beliefs at the same time (proof enough our brains are not rational machines!). There are more steps needed to make the conclusion sound.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think it's fair to say that a person needs structured mystic beliefs to say they &quot;believe in magic&quot;. A person wouldn't necessarily need to have a belief about how magic works to believe it exists.<br /> <br /> For example: it's common superstition among mantainers to believe a machine or location is &quot;out to get them&quot; or angry at them somehow. That doesn't necessarily mean they have a complex believe structure that details how the machines are doing wibbly magic to break in unlikely ways or make their day shit, but they believe that the objects are doing a general &quot;something&quot;.<br /> <br /> In the same vein, Many people will believe superstitions where they believe &quot;[Thing] causes [Effect]&quot; without any understanding of how or why, but that doesn't diminish the belief in the slightest.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480190#post1480190\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Because this is factually backwards. We develop non-rational and non-scientific behaviors as soon as we have a functional enough brain to do so. There is no &quot;rationality&quot; or &quot;science&quot; part of our brain. Rational and scientific thinking are trained skills.</div> </div> </div> </div>That was my point?<br /> <br /> I was saying that irrational behavior was a product of behaviors rooted deeply in the same things that make us human in the first place and despite Actively Trying to train people to unlearn those behaviors with education, people tend to just re-mold those irrational beliefs and behaviors around those new ideas.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480190#post1480190\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Superstitious athletes outperform non-superstitious athletes by a significant margin: because superstition is regularly used by athletes to unlock neural pathways reserved for physical expenditures during life or death situations, and then apply those to sports (decidedly not life or death most of the time).</div> </div> </div> </div>Do you have a source on that? Seems like an interesting topic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-05T13:06:12-07:00","id":1480199,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480194#post1480194\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think it's fair to say that a person needs structured mystic beliefs to say they &quot;believe in magic&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>You do get why I keep calling this equivocation, right? Regardless of how you feel about my position, you do understand what I mean with that word, yes?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That was my point? </div> </div> </div>It didn't really feel like that was your point? Especially the use of the phrase &quot;best education,&quot; without any markers to indicate that you don't think that is actually the best form of education.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I was saying that irrational behavior was a product of behaviors rooted deeply in the same things that make us human in the first place and despite Actively Trying to train people to unlearn those behaviors with education, people tend to just re-mold those irrational beliefs and behaviors around those new ideas. </div> </div> </div>See, like this? This is an acknowledgement that it probably isn't the best educational process available that wasn't present before.<br /> <br /> I say as this as someone that's actively written biology curricula for teachers in highly religious communities: &quot;actively trying to train people to unlearn,&quot; is horrible pedagogy. That's not how you effectively educate humans. You have to build on what they know and believe, and guide that remolding process instead of trying to force it. If you walk into a classroom full of kids who's parents have bumper stickers on their trucks to the extend of evolution is blasphemy, you are never going to win by trying to get them to &quot;unlearn&quot; anything.<br /> <br /> You meet them where they are. If it's a rural agricultural area, the majority of those students absolutely understand animal husbandry or selective breeding in crops. They already know how evolution works better than most rich suburban kids do, because how evolution works is why their families can make a living. They just need someone to connect the dots between humans breeding organisms together to get the results humans want, and what happens when nature picks who breeds with who.<br /> <br /> Crappy educational practices having results that aren't the intention of the educational process is because it was not the best education. It doesn't effectively make a point about human psychology.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Do you have a source on that? Seems like an interesting topic. </div> </div> </div>I haven't had access to paywalled academic journals in years, which impedes my ability to re-find and share the more recent stuff out there on the evolutionary benefits of superstition.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-05T13:31:52-07:00","id":1480200,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480199#post1480199\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You do get why I keep calling this equivocation, right?</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly no. I don't really see where anyone is using vague language to hide the truth/their point? I feel like I was personally making myself as clear as possible, but that seems to not be the case.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480199#post1480199\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It didn't really feel like that was your point? Especially the use of the phrase &quot;best education,&quot; without any markers to indicate that you don't think that is actually the best form of education.</div> </div> </div> </div>That seems a bit of a pedantic argument that willfully misses my point? I didn't mean &quot;best education&quot; in the under the definition of &quot;best possible education in all realities&quot; I meant it in the sense of &quot;best we currently have&quot; and by &quot;currently have&quot; I mean what's currently publicly available. Most of the times I've encountered this phrase, this is how it's been used.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480199#post1480199\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I say as this as someone that's actively written biology curricula for teachers in highly religious communities: &quot;actively trying to train people to unlearn,&quot; is horrible pedagogy. That's not how you effectively educate humans. You have to build on what they know and believe, and guide that remolding process instead of trying to force it. If you walk into a classroom full of kids who's parents have bumper stickers on their trucks to the extend of evolution is blasphemy, you are never going to win by trying to get them to &quot;unlearn&quot; anything.<br /> <br /> You meet them where they are. If it's a rural agricultural area, the majority of those students absolutely understand animal husbandry or selective breeding in crops. They already know how evolution works better than most rich suburban kids do, because how evolution works is why their families can make a living. They just need someone to connect the dots between humans breeding organisms together to get the results humans want, and what happens when nature picks who breeds with who.<br /> <br /> Crappy educational practices having results that aren't the intention of the educational process is because it was not the best education. It doesn't effectively make a point about human psychology.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not an expert so I won't comment on this subject.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480199#post1480199\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I haven't had access to paywalled academic journals in years, which impedes my ability to re-find and share the more recent stuff out there on the evolutionary benefits of superstition.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's a website called Scihub where scientist and professors post free versions their research articles for education and research purposes. It also may or may not exist because science journals refuse to pay researchers for their work while simultaneously leeching off it, and the website may or may not have been built as a middle finger to a parasitic system that wouldn't look out of place in the camarilla.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-05T14:49:35-07:00","id":1480210,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480200#post1480200\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly no.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equivocation\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/static/images/icons/enwiki-25.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equivocation\" target=\"_blank\"> Equivocation - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equivocation\" target=\"_blank\"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equivocation </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> &quot;In logic, equivocation ('calling two different things by the same name') is an informal fallacy resulting from the use of a particular word/expression in multiple senses within an argument.<br /> <br /> It is a type of ambiguity that stems from a phrase having two or more distinct meanings, not from the grammar or structure of the sentence.&quot;<br /> <br /> What I'm saying is, you and Bluecho are blurring the lines between phrases like, &quot;magical thinking,&quot; and &quot;believe in magic&quot; and magic as a mystic practice in your posts in a fashion that creates ambiguity instead of clarity. We're not just talking about why irrational and non-science based thoughts persist in human culture, we're talking about how the way humans think translates into the fictional reality of a game where belief matters to the manifestation of ritual mystic practices actually having tangible results non-scientific results.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That seems a bit of a pedantic argument that willfully misses my point? </div> </div> </div>Willful implies things I don't think feel are terribly productive.<br /> <br /> This is a pure text medium. I don't have facial expressions, intonations, body posture, and all those other cues to help judge intended meaning. I took your words are face value because I didn't see any reason not to. You didn't use things like scare quotes, or make a point that would require a reevaluation of the face value to be read charitably.<br /> <br /> Call it whatever you want. But I'm trying to give you feedback on how we can communicate more effectively: I didn't get any signal from your statement to read it in a different fashion. While I certainly bring my own biases into the process, I can't proactively change your posts for you to better express what's in your head in a fashion that makes sense to my head. I'm pointing to something and saying, &quot;this is why I responded the way I did.&quot; So, you know, we can try to refine our mutual interpretations of each other.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I didn't mean &quot;best education&quot; in the under the definition of &quot;best possible education in all realities&quot; I meant it in the sense of &quot;best we currently have&quot; and by &quot;currently have&quot; I mean what's currently publicly available. Most of the times I've encountered this phrase, this is how it's been used. </div> </div> </div>Which is a bit of a dilemma. What you described is not &quot;the best we have of what's currently publicly available.&quot; It hasn't been for decades. Obviously lots of people get bad educations anyway, but all these layers of unspoken context still result in a definition that doesn't change my response. What I said applies equally to what you're saying you meant, as it does to, &quot;best possible education in all realities.&quot;<br /> <br /> Trying to get people to &quot;unlearn&quot; is neither ideal, nor current best practices. My issue of you treating it as &quot;best&quot; remains in either case.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There's a website called Scihub where scientist and professors post free versions their research articles for education and research purposes. It also may or may not exist because science journals refuse to pay researchers for their work while simultaneously leeching off it, and the website may or may not have been built as a middle finger to a parasitic system that wouldn't look out of place in the camarilla. </div> </div> </div>Well, then you certainly don't need my help searching it then, do you? I'm not sure me searching a website for you is really that efficient when you have all the keywords you need already.<br /> <br /> Please remember when discussing the corrupting influence of conservative institutions that the US education system is hellish. I have seen a coworker suspended for a Facebook post of them holding a sealed bottle of hot sauce to their lips for looking too much like chugging a minibar bottle of alcohol, and another had her career ruined for an Instagram post of her holding a red plastic cup at a party and being unable to prove what was in it, so it was justified that to assume it was an illegal beverage for her age at the time, and thus she was an inappropriate role model for children.<br /> <br /> Yes I have modified details of both stories to provide some level of obfuscation from people that might try to pin more about me down. Because I don' want to get fired because someone manages to go through the effort of putting together my public social media profile and my RPG profile, because I would like to be able to enjoy my games without worrying about uptight twits that think I can't be a profession when I'm on the clock if I have hobbies they don't like. People in my position don't get things like tenure to protect us if we participate in &quot;subversive&quot; activities.<br /> <br /> So, yeah. I'm glad to have spark interest in a topic for you. But I'm not exactly happy with your choices about how to approach asking about sources.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-05T17:37:06-07:00","id":1480223,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480210#post1480210\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What I'm saying is, you and Bluecho are blurring the lines between phrases like, &quot;magical thinking,&quot; and &quot;believe in magic&quot; and magic as a mystic practice in your posts in a fashion that creates ambiguity instead of clarity. We're not just talking about why irrational and non-science based thoughts persist in human culture, we're talking about how the way humans think translates into the fictional reality of a game where belief matters to the manifestation of ritual mystic practices actually having tangible results non-scientific results.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay. To be a crystal clear as possible, I'm saying the widespread belief in superstition as a generality would make paradox function much differently than it does in mage if consensus was the biggest factor in making paradox happen.<br /> <br /> I'm not saying that everyone is secretly practicing the same belief system as the verbena or order of hermes. I also don't believe people Need to have the same paradigm as a mage or even Understand how a mage does magic to 100% believe the mage can do it.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480210#post1480210\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Which is a bit of a dilemma. What you described is not &quot;the best we have of what's currently publicly available.&quot; It hasn't been for decades. Obviously lots of people get bad educations anyway, but all these layers of unspoken context still result in a definition that doesn't change my response. What I said applies equally to what you're saying you meant, as it does to, &quot;best possible education in all realities.&quot;<br /> <br /> Trying to get people to &quot;unlearn&quot; is neither ideal, nor current best practices. My issue of you treating it as &quot;best&quot; remains in either case.</div> </div> </div> </div>Can we at least agree that my original point in that comment was to try to communicate is that it's common practice to try to make people unlearn the behaviors that lead to superstitious beliefs and that said common practice is largely unsuccessful? This tangent has dragged on for too long.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480210#post1480210\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, then you certainly don't need my help searching it then, do you?</div> </div> </div> </div>It would be helpful if you could give me the name of the research paper, but no, I can still find that on my own. I wasn't asking for you to search it up for me.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-05T18:43:36-07:00","id":1480230,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480223#post1480223\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Okay. To be a crystal clear as possible, I'm saying the widespread belief in superstition as a generality would make paradox function much differently than it does in mage if consensus was the biggest factor in making paradox happen.</div> </div> </div> </div>Consensus isn't the biggest factor in making Paradox happen. Consensus makes the coincidental/vulgar divide happen.<br /> <br /> I fail to see how modern lingering superstitions impact this. Just because people yell at their computer to work doesn't mean they'd believe it to be mere coincidence if you drew the spirit of your computer into the material plane to chastise it into behaving, vs. yelling at it as a show, mashing some keys, and then suddenly it works because apparently you made the box do its job if you hit enough buttons just right.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I also don't believe people Need to have the same paradigm as a mage or even Understand how a mage does magic to 100% believe the mage can do it. </div> </div> </div>The metaphysics of the setting don't allow for people to believe that magic is possible without also believing in a magical paradigm. You have to at least 100% believe in a paradigm, even if not the same paradigm, so you can filter in a reason on why something is possible. Someone that 100% believes in Hermetic magic can explain lots of magical practices within that lens, but not everything, and thus some things are still going to be vulgar to them (aka they'd be members of a Hermetic flavored Reality Zone).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Can we at least agree that my original point in that comment was to try to communicate is that it's common practice to try to make people unlearn the behaviors that lead to superstitious beliefs and that said common practice is largely unsuccessful? </div> </div> </div>If that's what you want to say, fine. I don't really know what something so utterly vague (&quot;commonplace&quot; is effectively meaningless in your phrasing) is meant to accomplish for the discussion though.<br /> <br /> None of this actually justifies your original statement about how education interacts with things like superstition, and toes very closely to be extremely insulting towards teachers and other education professionals as it relies on enough people spending years of their lives training in best practices to toss all of that out to do purposefully shitty jobs often enough to be commonplace.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-05T19:10:39-07:00","id":1480233,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480230#post1480230\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Consensus isn't the biggest factor in making Paradox happen. Consensus makes the coincidental/vulgar divide happen.</div> </div> </div> </div>So your interpretation of the setting is that paradox is not caused by the consensus?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480230#post1480230\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I fail to see how modern lingering superstitions impact this. Just because people yell at their computer to work doesn't mean they'd believe it to be mere coincidence if you drew the spirit of your computer into the material plane to chastise it into behaving, vs. yelling at it as a show, mashing some keys, and then suddenly it works because apparently you made the box do its job if you hit enough buttons just right.</div> </div> </div> </div>Because both would look the same from an outside lens? A dreamspeaker isn't necessarily going to do a big song and dance to draw out the spirit of a computer to get it working, they could literally go through the exact same actions as a normal dude yelling at/smacking her computer with the only difference being that the dreamspeaker rolls her Arete and has an underlying paradigm that she's smacking the spirit back into behaving.<br /> <br /> Not to mention the fact that normal people who do that don't necessarily do it for show, on some level many genuinely believe that yelling at/hitting the computer will make it work again(there's even a term for it &quot;percussive maintenance&quot;).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480230#post1480230\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The metaphysics of the setting don't allow for people to believe that magic is possible without also believing in a magical paradigm. You have to at least 100% believe in a paradigm, even if not the same paradigm, so you can filter in a reason on why something is possible. Someone that 100% believes in Hermetic magic can explain lots of magical practices within that lens, but not everything, and thus some things are still going to be vulgar to them (aka they'd be members of a Hermetic flavored Reality Zone).</div> </div> </div> </div>This I completely disagree with and I do not believe it's part of mages metaphysics. You don't need a fully detailed magical sub-system in your head to believe in something.<br /> <br /> Besides, that wouldn't really work for an explanation either, it still runs into the issue that anyone following a religious paradigm wouldn't count as a sleeper.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-05T20:33:59-07:00","id":1480250,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480233#post1480233\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So your interpretation of the setting is that paradox is not caused by the consensus?</div> </div> </div> </div>That's not his interpretation, that's the reality of the setting at least since M20 and arguably since <i>Revised</i>. Since there are things, be them called Cosmological Constants or Earthly Foundations, not to mention Botches, that induce Paradox regardless of Vulgarity, it is clear that Paradox exists despite the Consensus. And since even before that Paradox's chances and effects did not respond in any way to beliefs outside the belief of the affected Mage itself, except specifically for a quite binary factor of Vulgarity, we can infer that this effect, instead of crucial to Paradox, is just an added layer. More so when considering that Botches, not Vulgarity, are the oldest cause of Paradox.<br /> <br /> By chances and effects, I mean that the chance of something causing Paradox does not fluctuate with alignment to Consensus, or normalcy, as much as it fluctuates with other factors like Arete rolls and Sphere levels. And by effect, the fact that Paradox neither reinforces reality nor varies in nature/form of effect based on common belief, but at most in the Paradigm and nature of the magical effect that caused it.<br /> <br /> So while in the lore it is <i>possible</i> that Paradox is ultimately born from Consensus, and this at least was for a long time a default assumption between the Traditions (or at least the Hermetics) and, arguably, the authors, this is not a direct and necessary truth even for the Setting As Written, just one interpretation with enough counter arguments built in.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-05T20:51:28-07:00","id":1480252,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480250#post1480250\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So while in the lore it is <i>possible</i> that Paradox is ultimately born from Consensus, and this at least was for a long time a default assumption between the Traditions (or at least the Hermetics) and, arguably, the authors, this is not a direct and necessary truth even for the Setting As Written, just one interpretation with enough counter arguments built in.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was generally confused at that. In the context of the thread, I thought <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> was arguing against the idea that &quot;consensus isn't sufficient to explain paradox&quot;, but when they stated:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480230#post1480230\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Consensus isn't the biggest factor in making Paradox happen. Consensus makes the coincidental/vulgar divide happen.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was thrown for a loop. I'm 100% in agreement that consensus does not 1 for 1 equal paradox/reality/etc like the traditions claim, but now I'm lost on what the point of the argument even was to begin with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-05T21:23:50-07:00","id":1480259,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480252#post1480252\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">now I'm lost on what the point of the argument even was to begin with.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know, he was agreeing with your general point from the beginning, basically no one here disagrees with it. He disagreed with details and examples used.<br /> <br /> A lot of it was you using pedagogy-related analogies that were wrong and really get under his skin because he is a pedagogue himself, similar to how I get more easily aggravated when lay people make wrong analogies using psychology. It had nothing to do with actually disagreeing with the core point, unless he also misunderstood you.<br /> <br /> Oh, and the definition of Magical-Thinking. Correcting definitions was a thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":5826,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","user":"monteparnas","date":".  Last line added. 05-06-2022, 12:25 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-05T22:50:42-07:00","id":1480263,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's not his interpretation, that's the reality of the setting at least since M20 and arguably since <i>Revised</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would argue since even before that.<br /> <br /> Paradox in the Umbra was always a thing (Coincidental can still Botch), and the Consensus doesn't have much of a say there.<br /> <br /> The difference of Reality(tm) and &quot;consensual reality&quot; it's from 1e and, if I'm not misremembering, the idea that Paradox was a reaction to mages damaging or tearing Reality - or, the Telluriam - was already on 2e.<br /> <br /> I think that's a very important hairsplit: It's the idea that Revised uses to compare the Storm with a Backlash. Even when the rules have *nothing* in common, both are reactions to mages trashing reality, and reality pushing back.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was thrown for a loop. I'm 100% in agreement that consensus does not 1 for 1 equal paradox/reality/etc like the traditions claim..</div> </div> </div> </div>The Traditions don't claim that. The Traditions claim that humanity has the power to change reality, and maybe even Reality (but maybe only if they Mass Awaken/Ascend). Neatly summarized on M20 as the Paradigm: &quot;We Are Gods in Disguise&quot;<br /> <br /> They also claim that a lot of things fall outside the purview of the Consensus (the One, the Pure Ones, the Avatars, etc...these are all parts of the pre-revised Lore pointing towards a grander Reality than the &quot;consensual reality&quot;. Before Revised all of this was basically OOC &quot;The Truth&quot;, after Revised this is treated as part of the shared mythology of the Traditions).<br /> <br /> There needs to be a gray space between the Consensus 1 for 1 equaling Paradox and Reality - which was, perhaps, never a thing - and Belief <i>influencing </i>Paradox, and perhaps Reality (or at least some of it's manifestations) to varying degrees.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Because both would look the same from an outside lens? A dreamspeaker isn't necessarily going to do a big song and dance to draw out the spirit of a computer to get it working, they could literally go through the exact same actions as a normal dude yelling at/smacking her computer with the only difference being that the dreamspeaker rolls her Arete and has an underlying paradigm that she's smacking the spirit back into behaving. </div> </div> </div>I've &quot;punished&quot; dice to get better results. I don't <i>really </i>believe on it, but I would do it anyway. One could say I have those lingering &quot;beliefs&quot;, that perhaps even cause kind of an involuntary, sort of, &quot;magical thinking&quot; when I roll good afterwards and feel the punishment worked (or by the fact I would agree to the practice in the first place to correct a bad streak).<br /> <br /> But..<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Just because people yell at their computer to work doesn't mean they'd believe it to be mere coincidence<b> if you drew the spirit of your computer into the material plane</b> to chastise it into behaving...</div> </div> </div> </div>... if a screaming Dice Ghost would appear like on a Ghostbuster movie: I would be shocked, and probably disbelieve of my own experience afterwards - also would probably become afraid of dice. It certainly wouldn't be what I would expect to happen!<br /> <br /> Of course it's going to be Vulgar","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-06-2022, 02:00 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-06T06:54:42-07:00","id":1480284,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480263#post1480263\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Traditions don't claim that. The Traditions claim that humanity has the power to change reality, and maybe even Reality (but maybe only if they Mass Awaken/Ascend). Neatly summarized on M20 as the Paradigm: &quot;We Are Gods in Disguise&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Caveat: the Order of Hermes <b>does</b> formally believe that Consensus, or at least Human Will, equates to Reality.<br /> <br /> This is not a uniform or universal belief even among them, there's a lot of exceptions and nuance, but it is a foundational notion both for the Order and, due to their influence, to the Traditions as a whole, as a unified political body, and this belief <b>is</b> the source of the more amenable and poetic phrases like &quot;We Are Gods in Disguise&quot;.<br /> <br /> It isn't by any measure an actual majority opinion, but it is the most influential piece of Paradigm. IC because the OoH found it the best unifying theory for Magic, something they needed to give metaphysical weight to their Order, and later to the Traditions. OoC because it is the actual foundation of Telema and most other orders of modern Hermeticism the game was based on.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-06T12:13:36-07:00","id":1480302,"title":"","content":"Maybe, but<b> I think offering that nuance would be a very good idea </b>, when talking on a thread where the context it's something across the lines of &quot;that belief doesn't make any sense with the setting, insofar as the setting it's based on the real world, *we would be living on a very different real world/the setting should be very different* if it holded any water&quot; ( <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"6383\" data-vbnamecard=\"6383\">Prometheas</a> : Ain't that the gist of it? )<br /> <hr /><br /> I know not enough to speak about that, but about the OoH: The Trad Book(Revised) has a very neat, altrough also very christian-ish (Gnostic?, <i>RL </i>Hermetic?), explanation - that's obviously not universal because not all of the Order it's monotheistic, but it's the Trad:Book way of explaining the Paradigm, and thus shouldn't be given the cold shoulder when talking about &quot;the most influential piece of Paradigm&quot; for the faction<br /> <br /> My interpretation of it:<br /> <br /> In short, God created the universe, and then created Humans at his image - but in such a way that humans have, at least potentially, the same power as God. This is the reason given for human will to be able to rule over Reality. BUT, this potential it's not immediately realized. It has to be cultivated. One needs to study, practice, and earn that power, that Will.<br /> <br /> A Mage - a single mage - could be able to define Reality (that's the point behind their ideas of Ascencion). A &quot;City of Pymander&quot; (A place/world were everyone it's Awakened, and practicing) *would* be able to define the entirety of Reality.<br /> <br /> Human Will *can* define Reality to a point where the difference could be moot, sure, but extremely powerful Wizards (and, maybe, G'd. Is there a difference?) can have had a say in how the world works that, why not?, could pull above and beyond any Consensus <i>the </i><i>sheep, or perhaps it would be better to say the ignorant?: the human that hasn't realized the ways of Magick, </i>may have come up to. And, so, in my opinion one could say the difference it's still (or, at least, can be) relevant<br /> <br /> I would imagine some spin of this should be able to, more or less, accomodate the belief the &quot;Telema in OoH&quot; has (arguably, the belief behind all of Mage. Esp. older editions) to <i>Revised </i>and beyond.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-06-2022, 08:02 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-06T16:55:21-07:00","id":1480324,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">These Telema people must be really dense, if they can't note the obvious and evident discrepancy between their beliefs and the world :P<br /> \u200b So obvious that even a *fictional setting* it's wrong to base it's premises on it, and pretend any semblance of happening on a &quot;world similar to our own&quot;. :P</div> </div> </div> </div><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar5826_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5826\" data-vbnamecard=\"5826\">monteparnas</a> I fell a bit sorry about that irony: I imagine that's not your point. The point has to do with it failing to explain the <i>setting </i>in some very specific ways, no? (like all Paradigms do?) ...<br /> <br /> With all that &quot;Megachurches and Belief&quot;, &quot;Orient and Belief&quot;, &quot;the Ancient World and Belief&quot;, &quot;Magical Thinking and Belief&quot; ... that &quot;train of tougth&quot; I'm <i>really</i> directing my irony at, it's also a thing. Isn't it?. And I have some problems with that train, but could've expressed that concern a lot better than misconstruing that way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-06-2022, 08:02 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-06T23:51:41-07:00","id":1480349,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1479349-st-theories-why-did-magic-start-to-fail-in-da-mage?p=1480324#post1480324\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar5826_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5826\" data-vbnamecard=\"5826\">monteparnas</a> I fell a bit sorry about that irony: I imagine that's not your point. The point has to do with it failing to explain the <i>setting </i>in some very specific ways, no? (like all Paradigms do?) ...</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not offended as much as I have some problem fully understanding the irony itself, but that's on me.<br /> <br /> My point with Thelema (I was spelling it wrong) was exactly that: not to disagree with your previous post on what the Traditions claim, but just to point out where this specific Paradigm stands. Thelema is explicitly one of the main inspirations for the game, as it was for Ars Magica, regardless of how much sense we may actually see on it in the context of the setting (or outside it, for that matter). Not only that, it is also one of the main specific inspirations for the Order of Hermes, as an RL hermetic order founded by the most famous modern occultist.<br /> <br /> So while it isn't the only inspiration or anything, it is the most directly relevant one. Most of OoH magical theory and structure, as long as overall structure of the Traditions/OoH's influence on said structure, is OoC based on modern Hermeticism and Thelema in particular.<br /> <br /> <b>But</b> it must also be contextualized for this thread: Thelema itself has no relation at all with the idea that &quot;magic is dying&quot;. Quite the opposite, they consider the modern world as magical as always or even more. The setting has other sources of inspiration, like the already mentioned trope of human decaying with time and the End of History mood of the 90's. What we're specifically observing in this thread is the result of two specific inspirations for the setting not exactly meshing very well, maybe. <b>IF</b> you take this aspect of Hermetic Paradigm at face value, which as everyone I agree to not be the case, no matter how influential it was.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}